1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,242
Before any of it — before the building, before the
city, before the ships — there was

2
00:00:14,262 --> 00:00:18,946
a room that was not a room, and a voice in it
that had never needed a mouth.

3
00:00:20,097 --> 00:00:28,217
Selection. Eight billion candidates reviewed.
Criterion: will, unbroken at the moment

4
00:00:28,237 --> 00:00:30,515
of death. Two finalists.

5
00:00:31,106 --> 00:00:36,981
The first finalist died in a concrete room under a
ruined city in the spring of nineteen

6
00:00:37,002 --> 00:00:42,743
forty-five. He had begun the largest war in human
history and presided over the murder

7
00:00:42,764 --> 00:00:48,439
of millions of people, and when the armies of four
nations closed on the last streets

8
00:00:48,459 --> 00:00:52,055
he still held, he killed himself
rather than be taken.

9
00:00:52,841 --> 00:00:58,517
History took his measure and found nothing in him
worth keeping. The System was not looking

10
00:00:58,536 --> 00:01:03,210
for anything worth keeping. It was looking for
something that did not bend.

11
00:01:03,612 --> 00:01:09,417
The second finalist died sixty-four years later, in
a different world from this one, in

12
00:01:09,436 --> 00:01:15,376
the throat of an African volcano. A scientist who had
rebuilt his own body around a virus

13
00:01:15,396 --> 00:01:21,201
of his own design. A man who had betrayed every
institution that ever employed him, and

14
00:01:21,222 --> 00:01:26,290
who had come within an hour of rewriting the
human species in his own image.

15
00:01:26,691 --> 00:01:32,331
Two missiles found him in the lava. He was thinking,
as the fire took him, that he had

16
00:01:32,352 --> 00:01:33,781
simply moved too soon.

17
00:01:34,509 --> 00:01:40,460
The System weighed them the way a jeweller weighs two
stones. Not for beauty. For hardness.

18
00:01:41,610 --> 00:01:49,915
Merge. Host: Wesker, Albert. Will, body, intellect.
Passenger: secondary will, non-dominant,

19
00:01:49,936 --> 00:01:57,969
advisory. Rationale: the host has never had an
audience. The passenger was never anything

20
00:01:57,990 --> 00:01:58,423
else.

21
00:01:59,013 --> 00:02:05,043
And then, because the System had a sense of theatre
that it would never admit to, it did

22
00:02:05,063 --> 00:02:09,991
not put him in a hospital bed, or an alley,
or a chair in an empty room.

23
00:02:10,393 --> 00:02:11,752
It gave him a house.

24
00:02:12,151 --> 00:02:18,254
On the south bank of the Christina River, in the last
minute before six o'clock on a Monday

25
00:02:18,275 --> 00:02:24,039
morning, there was a parking apron. Cracked asphalt.
Two dumpsters. A chain-link fence

26
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:29,893
with a hole in it that the neighbourhood had been
using as a shortcut for eleven years.

27
00:02:30,294 --> 00:02:36,175
A man named Dale Moreno jogged past it at five
fifty-seven, as he did four mornings a

28
00:02:36,194 --> 00:02:41,313
week. He remembers the dumpsters. He is quite
certain about the dumpsters.

29
00:02:41,712 --> 00:02:45,872
At five fifty-eight, the parking apron
stopped being a parking apron.

30
00:02:46,274 --> 00:02:52,022
There was no sound. No flash, no wind, no shudder in
the ground. A six-storey building

31
00:02:52,043 --> 00:02:58,061
of white stone and black glass simply occupied the
space where nothing had been — complete

32
00:02:58,081 --> 00:03:04,098
to the coffee rings on the desks and the dust on top
of the doorframes. Above the entrance

33
00:03:04,117 --> 00:03:10,002
sat a crimson mark: six curved blades folded into a
circle, like an iris, or the shutter

34
00:03:10,021 --> 00:03:11,342
of a camera closing.

35
00:03:11,742 --> 00:03:18,233
Dale Moreno came back past it at six twenty. He did not
stop. He did not slow down. Somewhere

36
00:03:18,252 --> 00:03:24,812
in the machinery behind his eyes a small correction was
made, quietly, without his permission,

37
00:03:24,834 --> 00:03:28,592
and he thought: they finally
finished that renovation.

38
00:03:28,992 --> 00:03:30,703
There had been no renovation.

39
00:03:31,103 --> 00:03:36,933
In the City of Wilmington's property system, the
deed had been on file since 1998. The

40
00:03:36,954 --> 00:03:42,716
power company had twenty-eight years of billing
history. A Delaware corporation named

41
00:03:42,735 --> 00:03:48,770
Umbrella Research Division Seven had been paying
property taxes on that parcel for longer

42
00:03:48,789 --> 00:03:54,687
than anyone in the assessor's office had worked
there, and when a clerk opened the file

43
00:03:54,707 --> 00:04:00,469
that morning for an unrelated reason, she looked at
it for four seconds and closed it

44
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:04,144
again, because there was nothing in
it that was wrong.

45
00:04:04,544 --> 00:04:10,251
This is the first thing to understand about what
happened to this world. It did not begin

46
00:04:10,271 --> 00:04:14,432
with a monster. It began with paperwork
that was already perfect.

47
00:04:14,834 --> 00:04:20,612
On the sixth floor, in a corner office with a view
of the river, a man opened his eyes.

48
00:04:21,012 --> 00:04:26,769
He did not gasp. He did not grope at the arms of the
chair, or ask where he was, or do

49
00:04:26,788 --> 00:04:32,680
any of the other things a man does when the world has
moved underneath him. He sat still

50
00:04:32,699 --> 00:04:38,322
for twenty-six seconds with his hands resting on
the leather, and he took inventory.

51
00:04:38,723 --> 00:04:44,571
The body was his. Not the ruined thing the lava had
taken — the body from before, at its

52
00:04:44,591 --> 00:04:50,374
best. A heart that beat forty-one times a minute.
Hearing that picked out a fan running

53
00:04:50,394 --> 00:04:56,108
on the floor below. And underneath all of it,
patient and familiar, the virus. His own

54
00:04:56,129 --> 00:05:01,778
work. His own design. Still in there. Still
humming, like an engine left running in a

55
00:05:01,798 --> 00:05:02,711
locked garage.

56
00:05:03,112 --> 00:05:09,002
The suit was his. The coat over the chair-back was
his. On the blotter, folded, were the

57
00:05:09,021 --> 00:05:09,740
sunglasses.

58
00:05:10,141 --> 00:05:15,487
He put out a hand, and a pen he had knocked from the
desk was back between his fingers

59
00:05:15,507 --> 00:05:20,790
before it had fallen six inches. He looked at it
for a moment with something close to

60
00:05:20,810 --> 00:05:21,351
fondness.

61
00:05:21,872 --> 00:05:25,841
Well. That answers the
interesting question.

62
00:05:26,242 --> 00:05:31,875
And then there was the other thing. A second set of
memories sitting in his skull like

63
00:05:31,894 --> 00:05:37,659
a stranger in a waiting room: another country,
another century, a war he had not fought,

64
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:43,643
a death he had not died. He examined it the way he
would examine a contaminant in a culture

65
00:05:43,663 --> 00:05:48,442
— with distaste, with attention, and without
the smallest trace of alarm.

66
00:05:48,841 --> 00:05:54,834
Because Albert Wesker had been many things in two
lifetimes, and one of them was a man

67
00:05:54,855 --> 00:05:57,632
who was never frightened of information.

68
00:05:58,781 --> 00:06:07,766
Host confirmed. Albert Wesker. Director, Umbrella
Research Division Seven. Level one.

69
00:06:07,786 --> 00:06:11,793
Ten thousand Points. Tier one: Garage.

70
00:06:12,382 --> 00:06:13,252
Garage.

71
00:06:13,653 --> 00:06:14,913
Tier one of six.

72
00:06:15,312 --> 00:06:21,197
I am aware of what a first tier is. I am
remarking on the choice of word.

73
00:06:21,598 --> 00:06:23,189
The word is accurate.

74
00:06:23,588 --> 00:06:29,648
He looked at the panel for a long moment. And then,
for the first time in two lifetimes,

75
00:06:29,668 --> 00:06:33,518
Albert Wesker smiled at something
that had insulted him.

76
00:06:34,038 --> 00:06:39,507
Explain yourself. All of it. Start at the
top and leave nothing out.

77
00:06:39,908 --> 00:06:43,987
It took the System eighteen minutes. He
did not interrupt once.

78
00:06:45,137 --> 00:06:53,512
Status. Strength twenty-eight. Agility thirty-four.
Vitality twenty-six. Intelligence

79
00:06:53,533 --> 00:07:02,502
twenty-four. Presence sixteen. Will thirty. Viral
Affinity twelve. Ten is an average adult.

80
00:07:02,521 --> 00:07:05,266
Twenty is the human ceiling.

81
00:07:05,858 --> 00:07:11,588
Every number but one stood above the ceiling of his
species. He noted, without expression,

82
00:07:11,608 --> 00:07:12,737
which one did not.

83
00:07:13,257 --> 00:07:15,168
Presence. Sixteen.

84
00:07:15,567 --> 00:07:22,550
Presence measures your effect upon a crowd. You are
formidable in a room of one. You are

85
00:07:22,571 --> 00:07:25,098
cold in a room of four thousand.

86
00:07:25,499 --> 00:07:30,468
I have never had the slightest difficulty
making myself understood.

87
00:07:30,869 --> 00:07:33,899
Understood is not the same as followed.

88
00:07:34,927 --> 00:07:42,346
Points. Earned by the growth of the corporation,
completed missions, the devotion of those

89
00:07:42,365 --> 00:07:49,701
who serve you, acting with intelligence, and the
subjugation of rivals. Spent in the Shop

90
00:07:49,721 --> 00:07:50,858
and the Gacha.

91
00:07:52,076 --> 00:07:59,903
The Shop. Seven tabs open, three locked. Sixty-three
items available. One thousand two

92
00:07:59,923 --> 00:08:02,093
hundred and four locked.

93
00:08:02,682 --> 00:08:08,875
Facilities. Personnel. Vehicles. Viral. He read the
tab headings the way a starving man

94
00:08:08,894 --> 00:08:14,372
reads a menu, which is to say slowly, and while
pretending to be unimpressed.

95
00:08:14,891 --> 00:08:17,942
These items. Where do they arrive from?

96
00:08:18,343 --> 00:08:24,925
They do not arrive. They exist. You will choose a
location. At that location, the item

97
00:08:24,944 --> 00:08:29,379
will have existed for as long as it
needs to have existed.

98
00:08:29,778 --> 00:08:32,009
And the people who look at it?

99
00:08:32,408 --> 00:08:38,235
Those who cannot perceive this interface — which is
everyone — will remember it being

100
00:08:38,254 --> 00:08:44,631
built, will believe it has always stood there, or will
not notice it until they look directly

101
00:08:44,652 --> 00:08:50,547
at it, at which point they will assume they had
never happened to look before. Records

102
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:56,394
will agree with them. Witnesses will agree with
them. There will be no seam. You will

103
00:08:56,413 --> 00:09:00,038
be the only living creature who
knows the difference.

104
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:02,470
He was quiet for several seconds.

105
00:09:02,990 --> 00:09:09,691
Do you know how much of my last life I spent
building false histories for things that

106
00:09:09,711 --> 00:09:10,720
had to exist?

107
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:12,549
Thirty-one years.

108
00:09:12,950 --> 00:09:14,440
Thirty-one years.

109
00:09:15,470 --> 00:09:23,181
The Gacha. One pull, one thousand. A ten-pull, nine
thousand, with one Rare or better

110
00:09:23,201 --> 00:09:31,731
guaranteed. A Rare may be a specialist, an ability, or a
single biological weapon. A Legendary

111
00:09:31,751 --> 00:09:34,643
is not an item. It is a package.

112
00:09:35,863 --> 00:09:44,359
Roster. Eleven Absolute. Zero integrated. Personnel
acquired through this interface cannot

113
00:09:44,379 --> 00:09:52,119
disobey. Personnel hired by ordinary means possess
a loyalty value, and may leave.

114
00:09:52,710 --> 00:09:54,870
Eleven. He turned that number over.

115
00:09:55,389 --> 00:09:57,759
Eleven people are downstairs.

116
00:09:58,158 --> 00:10:05,187
Eleven people have worked in this building for between
two and nine years, depending which

117
00:10:05,207 --> 00:10:06,440
of them you ask.

118
00:10:06,841 --> 00:10:12,389
And last, the panel he had been waiting for without
knowing he was waiting for it, because

119
00:10:12,409 --> 00:10:16,661
the System had read him perfectly and had
saved the hook for the end.

120
00:10:17,811 --> 00:10:26,174
Main mission. Tier two: Company. A product worth
selling. Fifty staff. Fifty million dollars

121
00:10:26,195 --> 00:10:34,378
in assets. Reward: twenty-five thousand Points, the
Territory tab, and the Company banner.

122
00:10:34,969 --> 00:10:38,058
And when I reach the sixth tier?

123
00:10:38,457 --> 00:10:41,161
Then we will discuss what is above it.

124
00:10:41,562 --> 00:10:46,131
He stood, and took the sunglasses from the
blotter, and did not put them on yet.

125
00:10:46,652 --> 00:10:53,418
I built a company like this once. It took thirty
years, a great deal of money that was

126
00:10:53,438 --> 00:10:57,600
not mine, and a board of directors
I had to smile at.

127
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:58,812
Yes.

128
00:10:59,211 --> 00:11:00,761
How long this time?

129
00:11:01,162 --> 00:11:03,904
That is entirely a question about you.

130
00:11:04,303 --> 00:11:05,754
He walked his own house.

131
00:11:06,153 --> 00:11:12,001
Five floors of laboratory. Sequencing suites with
the machines still under dust covers.

132
00:11:12,022 --> 00:11:17,802
A cold room. A tissue-culture wing. An animal
facility that smelled of nothing at all.

133
00:11:17,822 --> 00:11:23,601
Glass everywhere, and behind the glass, equipment he
recognised from catalogues he had

134
00:11:23,621 --> 00:11:29,605
read in another world, in versions eighteen years
newer than the ones he had died beside.

135
00:11:30,004 --> 00:11:35,567
A freezer archive on the third floor held thirty
years of cell lines that had never been

136
00:11:35,587 --> 00:11:40,961
collected, from studies that had never been run, by
people who had never existed. The

137
00:11:40,981 --> 00:11:45,783
labels were handwritten. Some of them had been
corrected in a different pen.

138
00:11:46,303 --> 00:11:48,033
Even the mistakes.

139
00:11:48,433 --> 00:11:54,360
A three-level underground garage with nothing parked
in it. A loading dock. A boardroom

140
00:11:54,379 --> 00:12:00,647
with eleven chairs and a table polished so often that
the wood had gone soft at the corners.

141
00:12:01,048 --> 00:12:03,518
And at twenty to eight, they came to work.

142
00:12:03,918 --> 00:12:09,437
They did not trickle in. They arrived together, which
is the first thing about them that

143
00:12:09,456 --> 00:12:14,975
anybody sane would have found strange, and they
formed a line across the lobby as though

144
00:12:14,995 --> 00:12:18,628
they had been waiting all weekend for
permission to exist.

145
00:12:19,028 --> 00:12:24,707
Hale, chief of security: two metres of shaved head
and black tactical suit, flanked by

146
00:12:24,727 --> 00:12:30,341
two officers who stood exactly as he stood. Gus,
maintenance, in navy coveralls, with

147
00:12:30,361 --> 00:12:35,975
a tool belt and a ring of keys and eyes like a calm
pond. Mr. Pell, comptroller, thin

148
00:12:35,995 --> 00:12:41,741
and grey and spectacled, holding a folder. Dr. Lena
Voss and three junior scientists in

149
00:12:41,761 --> 00:12:47,774
white coats. And at the desk, a receptionist named
Marguerite, smiling the way a photograph

150
00:12:47,793 --> 00:12:48,238
smiles.

151
00:12:48,638 --> 00:12:54,979
Nobody fidgeted. Nobody checked a phone. Eleven pairs
of eyes came up to him at the same

152
00:12:54,999 --> 00:12:57,148
instant, like a flock turning.

153
00:12:57,668 --> 00:12:59,557
Good morning, Director.

154
00:12:59,956 --> 00:13:02,606
Wesker looked at them for a while.

155
00:13:03,128 --> 00:13:09,702
Mr. Hale. Your left-hand officer carries a sidearm he
has not drawn since it was issued,

156
00:13:09,722 --> 00:13:16,224
and a scar on his wrist that is four years older
than his employment. Who was he before

157
00:13:16,244 --> 00:13:17,798
he stood in my lobby?

158
00:13:18,197 --> 00:13:19,888
He was nothing, sir.

159
00:13:20,288 --> 00:13:24,538
Said pleasantly. Said the way another man
confirms a lunch order.

160
00:13:25,057 --> 00:13:29,008
Mr. Gus. How many doors are there
in this building?

161
00:13:29,408 --> 00:13:34,657
Three hundred and sixteen, Director. Three hundred
and seventeen if you count the one

162
00:13:34,677 --> 00:13:37,447
in the basement that doesn't go
anywhere yet.

163
00:13:37,846 --> 00:13:40,836
Wesker's head turned, very slightly.

164
00:13:41,355 --> 00:13:41,966
Yet.

165
00:13:42,365 --> 00:13:43,475
Yes, sir.

166
00:13:43,875 --> 00:13:49,346
And then, because he had never been willing to take
a thing on trust when he could test

167
00:13:49,365 --> 00:13:54,711
it instead, he did something quiet and
unforgivable. He looked at Marguerite, who was

168
00:13:54,730 --> 00:14:00,517
twenty-four years old and had a photograph of a dog on
her desk, and he said, in a perfectly

169
00:14:00,537 --> 00:14:01,274
level voice:

170
00:14:01,793 --> 00:14:08,004
Put your hand flat on the desk and hold it there.
I am going to break your finger.

171
00:14:08,403 --> 00:14:10,375
She put her hand flat on the desk.

172
00:14:11,048 --> 00:14:16,558
She did not hesitate. She did not look down at it.
She did not look at him. She simply

173
00:14:16,578 --> 00:14:22,153
laid her hand on the wood and waited, wearing the
same pleasant, patient expression she

174
00:14:22,173 --> 00:14:27,619
had worn thirty seconds earlier, while the dog in
the photograph went on being a dog.

175
00:14:28,019 --> 00:14:32,750
Nobody in the line moved to stop him.
Nobody in the line looked away.

176
00:14:33,490 --> 00:14:36,100
Thank you. Take your hand back.

177
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:42,765
She did. Wesker considered his own gloved hand for a
moment. Then he looked at the eleven

178
00:14:42,786 --> 00:14:48,769
of them, and something in him that had spent
thirty-one years managing ambitious men,

179
00:14:48,789 --> 00:14:55,057
frightened men, men who had to be bribed and
blackmailed and buried, settled at last into

180
00:14:55,076 --> 00:14:55,480
place.

181
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,250
Nobody has ever been able to give me this.

182
00:15:00,282 --> 00:15:08,798
Roster detail. Eleven Absolute personnel. Loyalty is
not a variable. They will not question,

183
00:15:08,817 --> 00:15:15,201
resign, testify or hesitate. They are not
machines. They can be lost.

184
00:15:15,791 --> 00:15:18,010
It was the last line he read twice.

185
00:15:18,409 --> 00:15:24,172
He spent the day reading. Pell's folder: the
corporation, the accounts, the leases, the

186
00:15:24,192 --> 00:15:30,020
thirty-year history of a company that had never done
anything and had never quite failed

187
00:15:30,041 --> 00:15:35,934
either. Voss's folder: four research programmes, none
of them going anywhere, all of them

188
00:15:35,955 --> 00:15:36,399
funded.

189
00:15:36,801 --> 00:15:42,590
By six he had the shape of it, and by six he
understood the joke the System had made at

190
00:15:42,610 --> 00:15:48,599
his expense. It had not given him a garage. It had
given him something worse — a perfectly

191
00:15:48,619 --> 00:15:54,408
respectable, perfectly solvent, perfectly invisible
research institute of no importance

192
00:15:54,428 --> 00:15:59,750
whatsoever. A door into the world, and nothing on
the other side of it but work.

193
00:16:00,149 --> 00:16:05,743
The other voice had been silent all day. Now, at the
window, in the grey light off the

194
00:16:05,763 --> 00:16:11,291
river, it stirred at last: an older voice, rougher,
sitting further back in the skull

195
00:16:11,311 --> 00:16:13,379
than any thought has a right to.

196
00:16:13,899 --> 00:16:14,999
Where are they?

197
00:16:15,399 --> 00:16:16,109
Who?

198
00:16:16,509 --> 00:16:24,466
The crowd. There is a hall downstairs with five
hundred chairs in it. You have no one

199
00:16:24,485 --> 00:16:25,874
to put in them.

200
00:16:26,274 --> 00:16:32,802
I have eleven people who would walk into a fire if I
pointed at it. That is not a crowd.

201
00:16:32,822 --> 00:16:33,918
That is a tool.

202
00:16:34,317 --> 00:16:40,076
A tool cannot carry you. Eleven people
cannot shout a name.

203
00:16:40,476 --> 00:16:48,110
Eleven people do not need to. That was always your
error. You built a country out of shouting,

204
00:16:48,129 --> 00:16:54,947
and it came down in twelve years, because a shout
is the only thing a shout can buy.

205
00:16:55,567 --> 00:16:57,393
And what will yours buy?

206
00:16:57,793 --> 00:16:58,942
Signatures.

207
00:16:59,341 --> 00:17:04,868
There was a silence then, and the narrator will tell
you plainly that it was not a friendly

208
00:17:04,888 --> 00:17:05,113
one.

209
00:17:05,511 --> 00:17:06,842
The other voice stirred.

210
00:17:07,363 --> 00:17:14,689
You will need a crowd eventually. When that day
comes you will not know how to stand in

211
00:17:14,709 --> 00:17:22,035
front of it. I watched you this morning with those
eleven. You are magnificent at close

212
00:17:22,055 --> 00:17:25,328
range, and at fifty metres you are ice.

213
00:17:25,728 --> 00:17:26,718
Sixteen.

214
00:17:27,116 --> 00:17:27,721
What?

215
00:17:28,121 --> 00:17:30,330
Nothing. Go to sleep.

216
00:17:30,730 --> 00:17:36,471
He did not say the rest of it aloud, which was that the
System had put a number on precisely

217
00:17:36,491 --> 00:17:42,045
the thing this passenger in his skull was good at —
and that he was going to have to make

218
00:17:42,065 --> 00:17:47,681
use of him, and that he expected to enjoy it about as
much as a surgeon enjoys a splinter.

219
00:17:48,079 --> 00:17:53,790
At eight o'clock he set the sunglasses on and looked
at his own reflection in the black

220
00:17:53,810 --> 00:17:59,521
window — over the river, over a city that had
rearranged its own memory that morning to

221
00:17:59,541 --> 00:18:02,420
make room for him, and had not
felt a thing.

222
00:18:02,819 --> 00:18:08,680
Somewhere below him, three hundred and seventeen
doors. One of them did not go anywhere

223
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:08,950
yet.

224
00:18:09,471 --> 00:18:12,299
Thirty-one years, last time.

225
00:18:12,701 --> 00:18:14,049
He turned off the light.

226
00:18:25,799 --> 00:18:31,619
He did not go home. There was, as it happened, a
home: a leased apartment eleven minutes

227
00:18:31,639 --> 00:18:37,393
away with his name on the mailbox and a refrigerator
somebody had stocked. He looked at

228
00:18:37,413 --> 00:18:40,711
the address on the key fob once
and never used it.

229
00:18:41,109 --> 00:18:46,711
Instead he spent the first night of his second life
going through his own building the

230
00:18:46,730 --> 00:18:52,461
way a burglar goes through a house — floor by floor,
drawer by drawer, taking a complete

231
00:18:52,481 --> 00:18:54,879
inventory of what he had been handed.

232
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:57,865
By four in the morning he knew
three things.

233
00:18:58,266 --> 00:19:04,049
The first: the equipment was excellent and the
science was garbage. Four programmes, all

234
00:19:04,069 --> 00:19:09,788
of them fundable, none of them going anywhere.
Somebody — no one, the System, a rule of

235
00:19:09,808 --> 00:19:15,724
the universe he had not yet learned the name of — had
furnished this place with everything

236
00:19:15,744 --> 00:19:19,154
a serious laboratory needs except a
reason to exist.

237
00:19:19,555 --> 00:19:24,586
The second: the freezer archive on the third floor
was worth more than the building.

238
00:19:24,984 --> 00:19:31,500
Thirty years of cell lines. Primate kidney lines,
human fibroblast lines, forty-one vials

239
00:19:31,520 --> 00:19:37,817
of something a technician who never lived had
labelled *ODD — DO NOT DISCARD* in 2004.

240
00:19:37,836 --> 00:19:44,204
Half of it was junk. The other half was the kind of
junk that a man with the right eyes

241
00:19:44,224 --> 00:19:46,776
could build a decade of product on.

242
00:19:47,174 --> 00:19:50,549
And the third thing: three hundred
and seventeen doors.

243
00:19:50,951 --> 00:19:57,062
On the lowest level of the garage, past the painted
bays that had never held a car, there

244
00:19:57,083 --> 00:20:03,126
was a wall. Grey, unremarkable, cold to the touch.
Behind it, according to every drawing

245
00:20:03,146 --> 00:20:08,501
in the building, there was Delaware — earth and
old riverbed and nothing else.

246
00:20:08,901 --> 00:20:13,892
Wesker stood in front of it for a long time
with his hands behind his back.

247
00:20:14,412 --> 00:20:16,481
Shop. Facilities.

248
00:20:17,510 --> 00:20:25,722
Facilities. Cold Archive Vault, twelve hundred.
Automated Sequencing Suite, two thousand

249
00:20:25,742 --> 00:20:33,767
five hundred. Containment Wing, B S L three, four
thousand. Laboratory Sublevel, B S L

250
00:20:33,787 --> 00:20:42,558
four — nine rooms, cold vault, freight lift — six
thousand. Production Plant, twelve thousand.

251
00:20:43,150 --> 00:20:48,826
Six thousand of his ten. More than half of
everything he had, spent in one motion, on

252
00:20:48,846 --> 00:20:50,098
the first full day.

253
00:20:50,499 --> 00:20:55,998
He did not hesitate for even a second, and this is
worth saying plainly, because most

254
00:20:56,018 --> 00:21:01,711
men in his position would have. Most men would have
bought the sequencing suite and kept

255
00:21:01,730 --> 00:21:07,230
a reserve. Most men would have waited to understand
the rules before betting the bank

256
00:21:07,250 --> 00:21:07,749
on them.

257
00:21:08,269 --> 00:21:10,759
Purchase. Place it here.

258
00:21:11,159 --> 00:21:12,691
Specify a depth.

259
00:21:13,092 --> 00:21:19,996
Directly beneath this floor. Lift access from the
garage, service stair to the loading

260
00:21:20,016 --> 00:21:23,942
dock, no entrance that a delivery
driver can see.

261
00:21:24,342 --> 00:21:31,280
Confirmed. The installation will complete between two
and four o'clock tomorrow morning.

262
00:21:31,681 --> 00:21:32,590
Why then?

263
00:21:32,990 --> 00:21:39,514
Because at that hour there is no one in this
building to have a memory adjusted, which

264
00:21:39,534 --> 00:21:40,353
is cheaper.

265
00:21:40,752 --> 00:21:46,243
He liked that answer. He liked it enormously. It was
the first time the System had told

266
00:21:46,263 --> 00:21:51,691
him something about its own machinery, and what it
had told him was that the machinery

267
00:21:51,711 --> 00:21:57,138
had a cost, and a cost meant a limit, and a limit
was a thing that could eventually be

268
00:21:57,159 --> 00:21:58,722
understood and exploited.

269
00:21:59,241 --> 00:22:02,412
Everything has a budget. Even you.

270
00:22:02,813 --> 00:22:05,723
Everything has a budget. Even you.

271
00:22:06,752 --> 00:22:17,211
Purchase. Laboratory Sublevel, B S L four. Six
thousand. Balance, four thousand. Designation:

272
00:22:17,230 --> 00:22:18,561
Sublevel B4.

273
00:22:19,152 --> 00:22:25,479
Gus was waiting by the lift when he came down in the
morning. Not standing in the lobby.

274
00:22:25,499 --> 00:22:31,608
Waiting by the lift, exactly where Wesker would
need him, having decided this without

275
00:22:31,628 --> 00:22:32,402
being told.

276
00:22:32,922 --> 00:22:36,159
Morning, Director. Lift's got a
fourth button.

277
00:22:36,557 --> 00:22:37,108
Does it.

278
00:22:37,508 --> 00:22:43,485
Yes, sir. B4. It's had one since the building went
up, only the contacts were dirty, so

279
00:22:43,505 --> 00:22:46,037
it never lit. I cleaned them Tuesday.

280
00:22:46,438 --> 00:22:47,357
A pause.

281
00:22:47,877 --> 00:22:53,339
I don't remember cleaning them Tuesday. But I did.
I've got the swab in my cart and the

282
00:22:53,359 --> 00:22:55,923
log's filled in, and it's my handwriting.

283
00:22:56,322 --> 00:23:02,936
Wesker looked at him with genuine interest — the
interest of a man examining an instrument

284
00:23:02,956 --> 00:23:06,032
he has just learned reports its own drift.

285
00:23:06,552 --> 00:23:09,083
Does that trouble you, Mr. Gus?

286
00:23:09,482 --> 00:23:11,226
No, sir. Should it?

287
00:23:11,625 --> 00:23:12,355
No.

288
00:23:12,755 --> 00:23:13,862
Then it doesn't.

289
00:23:14,262 --> 00:23:20,010
And that, the narrator would like to note, is the
whole of it. That exchange is the entire

290
00:23:20,030 --> 00:23:25,971
architecture of what Umbrella became, laid out in four
sentences beside a lift on a Wednesday

291
00:23:25,991 --> 00:23:31,420
morning by a man in coveralls who was six days old
and would have said the same thing

292
00:23:31,440 --> 00:23:33,021
with a knife in his hand.

293
00:23:33,423 --> 00:23:37,472
The lift went down past the third garage
level, and kept going.

294
00:23:37,873 --> 00:23:44,423
Nine rooms. A containment wing rated for organisms that
no ordinary company has any business

295
00:23:44,442 --> 00:23:50,778
owning. A cold vault with a hand-wheel door thirty
centimetres thick. A sequencing bench,

296
00:23:50,798 --> 00:23:57,205
an incubator bank, an autoclave, a shower-down
airlock, and a freight lift that officially

297
00:23:57,226 --> 00:24:00,563
served a storage mezzanine on
the second floor.

298
00:24:00,961 --> 00:24:06,601
The paint smelled of nothing. The steel was the
temperature of the surrounding earth.

299
00:24:06,621 --> 00:24:12,326
There was a service tag on the air handler dated
March 2019 and signed by a contractor

300
00:24:12,346 --> 00:24:18,185
in Newark who had been in business for forty years
and who would, if asked, remember the

301
00:24:18,205 --> 00:24:18,452
job.

302
00:24:18,851 --> 00:24:25,107
Wesker walked the length of it twice. At the far end
he stopped, and put one gloved hand

303
00:24:25,127 --> 00:24:28,601
flat against a containment door,
and stood there.

304
00:24:29,002 --> 00:24:32,393
From somewhere behind his eyes, the
passenger spoke.

305
00:24:32,912 --> 00:24:34,326
You are pleased.

306
00:24:34,726 --> 00:24:35,557
I am.

307
00:24:35,956 --> 00:24:37,989
It is a basement.

308
00:24:38,389 --> 00:24:45,135
It is nine rooms that appeared under a car park
because I decided they should. In my last

309
00:24:45,155 --> 00:24:51,598
life this took me four years, a shell company in
Liechtenstein and two men who had to

310
00:24:51,618 --> 00:24:53,499
be persuaded not to talk.

311
00:24:53,900 --> 00:25:01,318
And in mine, this was a thing that had to be built
by hand, in the open, where everyone

312
00:25:01,338 --> 00:25:03,286
could count the trucks.

313
00:25:03,684 --> 00:25:10,031
The narrator notes that this is very nearly the only
time the passenger will say anything

314
00:25:10,051 --> 00:25:13,394
wistful, and that Wesker ignored
it completely.

315
00:25:13,793 --> 00:25:17,913
Dr. Lena Voss had a problem, and had
had it for two years.

316
00:25:18,313 --> 00:25:24,502
Her programme was a viral vector: a hollowed-out,
harmless virus meant to carry a corrective

317
00:25:24,522 --> 00:25:30,915
gene into human cells. It worked in a dish. It did not
work in anything that had a bloodstream,

318
00:25:30,935 --> 00:25:36,788
and every animal run for two years had come back the
same way — a beautiful first week,

319
00:25:36,807 --> 00:25:40,163
then the effect falling off a
cliff, then nothing.

320
00:25:40,563 --> 00:25:46,884
The file was four hundred pages. Wesker read it in
the time it took Voss to make a cup

321
00:25:46,903 --> 00:25:48,653
of tea she did not want.

322
00:25:49,173 --> 00:25:50,884
Your vector is fine.

323
00:25:51,284 --> 00:25:55,473
Director, with respect, the vector is not
fine. Twenty-two runs.

324
00:25:55,873 --> 00:26:02,831
Twenty-two runs in which the payload was expressed at
eleven days and gone at twenty-eight.

325
00:26:02,851 --> 00:26:09,502
You concluded that your construct is unstable. Show
me the twenty-eight day immunology.

326
00:26:09,903 --> 00:26:13,333
She showed him. He looked at it for
perhaps nine seconds.

327
00:26:13,854 --> 00:26:21,063
There. Neutralising antibody against the capsid,
rising from day twelve. You are not losing

328
00:26:21,083 --> 00:26:27,894
expression. You are being cleared. The animal's
immune system is learning the shape of

329
00:26:27,914 --> 00:26:34,807
your delivery vehicle and taking it apart, and
because you re-dose on day fourteen, you

330
00:26:34,826 --> 00:26:38,223
are training it to do so
faster every time.

331
00:26:38,623 --> 00:26:41,213
We screened for pre-existing immunity —

332
00:26:41,613 --> 00:26:48,528
To the wild serotype. Not to yours. You built a
chimera and then tested for antibodies

333
00:26:48,548 --> 00:26:50,463
against its grandfather.

334
00:26:51,139 --> 00:26:56,877
The narrator would like to be precise about what
happened in that room, because it happened

335
00:26:56,898 --> 00:27:02,701
again and again for the next sixty-five years, in
laboratories and boardrooms and eventually

336
00:27:02,721 --> 00:27:06,307
in places without air, and it never
once stopped working.

337
00:27:06,708 --> 00:27:12,433
He did not raise his voice. He did not gloat, and
he did not explain twice. He simply

338
00:27:12,453 --> 00:27:18,316
picked up the one number in four hundred pages that
mattered, laid it on the table, and

339
00:27:18,336 --> 00:27:21,898
let the other person in the room
feel the floor move.

340
00:27:22,418 --> 00:27:29,164
Swap the capsid every dose. Three serotypes on
rotation, none of them related, all of

341
00:27:29,184 --> 00:27:36,009
them carrying the same payload. The immune system
cannot learn a face that never comes

342
00:27:36,029 --> 00:27:36,409
back.

343
00:27:36,807 --> 00:27:39,239
That's — that is four separate constructs.

344
00:27:39,639 --> 00:27:45,443
It is four separate constructs and five weeks.
Start tonight. You will have a working

345
00:27:45,463 --> 00:27:51,406
platform before Christmas, and the platform is worth
a great deal more than the therapy

346
00:27:51,427 --> 00:27:53,668
you were trying to build with it.

347
00:27:54,067 --> 00:28:00,174
Voss stood in the middle of her own laboratory
holding a cold cup of tea while a man she

348
00:28:00,194 --> 00:28:06,094
had worked for, as far as she could remember, since
2021, dismantled two years of her

349
00:28:06,114 --> 00:28:08,877
life and handed it back to her improved.

350
00:28:09,398 --> 00:28:11,068
Yes, Director.

351
00:28:11,467 --> 00:28:17,275
She did not resent it. That is what unsettled the
junior scientist who was listening from

352
00:28:17,295 --> 00:28:22,904
the corridor. She looked, if anything, grateful —
the way a lost person is grateful to

353
00:28:22,924 --> 00:28:26,177
the stranger who turns their map
the right way up.

354
00:28:27,327 --> 00:28:35,429
Points. Acting with intelligence: nine hundred.
Level two. Level three. Six stat points

355
00:28:35,449 --> 00:28:36,362
available.

356
00:28:36,951 --> 00:28:38,221
Intelligence.

357
00:28:38,621 --> 00:28:39,630
All six?

358
00:28:40,030 --> 00:28:46,476
All six. I am fast enough to catch a bullet and I
have nothing worth catching one for

359
00:28:46,496 --> 00:28:46,780
yet.

360
00:28:47,811 --> 00:28:52,648
Status update. Intelligence,
twenty-four to twenty-seven.

361
00:28:53,240 --> 00:28:58,961
He felt it happen. That is the detail he would
never describe to anyone, not once, in

362
00:28:58,980 --> 00:29:04,836
sixty-five years: that raising the number did not
feel like learning something. It felt

363
00:29:04,856 --> 00:29:08,889
like a room he had always lived in
acquiring another window.

364
00:29:09,288 --> 00:29:15,601
Two more purchases, small ones, from the Personnel
tab: laboratory technicians, six hundred

365
00:29:15,621 --> 00:29:16,438
Points each.

366
00:29:16,836 --> 00:29:22,741
They were in the building on Thursday morning. Human
resources had their files: one from

367
00:29:22,761 --> 00:29:28,531
Rutgers, one from Delaware State, both interviewed
in June by Dr. Voss, who remembered

368
00:29:28,552 --> 00:29:34,389
the interviews and could describe what they had
worn. Payroll had them. The coffee fund

369
00:29:34,409 --> 00:29:40,112
had them. One of them had a bicycle chained outside
with a lock that had been rusting

370
00:29:40,132 --> 00:29:40,987
for a season.

371
00:29:41,386 --> 00:29:47,057
Wesker watched them work for an hour from the
observation window, and noted that they

372
00:29:47,076 --> 00:29:53,148
were better than competent and worse than curious.
They did precisely what they were asked.

373
00:29:53,168 --> 00:29:55,356
They did not ask what it was for.

374
00:29:55,757 --> 00:29:58,927
The voice in his skull chose that
moment to arrive.

375
00:29:59,447 --> 00:30:00,951
You should tell them.

376
00:30:01,351 --> 00:30:02,422
Tell them what?

377
00:30:02,822 --> 00:30:09,444
What they are part of. Men work harder for a
cathedral than for a wage.

378
00:30:09,842 --> 00:30:15,853
These four cannot work less hard. They are
incapable of it. And the others —

379
00:30:16,253 --> 00:30:21,976
He was quiet for a moment, watching Voss through
the glass, bent over a bench at nine

380
00:30:21,996 --> 00:30:26,503
at night, rebuilding her own project with
the fervour of a convert.

381
00:30:27,023 --> 00:30:33,663
The others are being shown. People who are told a
plan begin to negotiate about the plan.

382
00:30:33,683 --> 00:30:38,753
People who are shown a miracle simply
follow it to see the next one.

383
00:30:39,153 --> 00:30:41,331
That is what a sermon is.

384
00:30:41,730 --> 00:30:49,342
No. A sermon is where you tell them in advance and
hope they believe you. I prefer to

385
00:30:49,362 --> 00:30:56,074
be believed retroactively. It costs less and
nobody has a chance to object.

386
00:30:56,476 --> 00:31:01,175
In the world outside, almost nothing happened,
which was exactly the point.

387
00:31:01,576 --> 00:31:07,972
The Delaware Business Ledger ran four lines in its
filings column: *Umbrella Research

388
00:31:07,992 --> 00:31:14,615
Division Seven (Wilmington) — registered capital
increase; new laboratory certification,

389
00:31:14,635 --> 00:31:21,335
BSL-4, pending state inspection.* Nobody read it. The
paper's own filings columnist could

390
00:31:21,355 --> 00:31:25,185
not have told you the company's
name an hour later.

391
00:31:25,586 --> 00:31:31,634
A state inspector's office in Dover received an
application for a containment certification,

392
00:31:31,653 --> 00:31:37,373
complete, correctly filled in, with a floor plan
that matched the building, and stamped

393
00:31:37,393 --> 00:31:41,264
it in eleven days, because there was
nothing wrong with it.

394
00:31:41,664 --> 00:31:47,275
And at the coffee shop across the street, the owner
told her Friday regular that the big

395
00:31:47,295 --> 00:31:52,712
white place had finally started hiring again, and
the regular said it was about time,

396
00:31:52,732 --> 00:31:56,615
and neither of them could have told you
what the company did.

397
00:31:57,014 --> 00:32:02,185
Late on Friday, Wesker carried the first of
the archive down himself.

398
00:32:02,586 --> 00:32:08,326
Forty-one vials, in a dry shipper, from the third
floor to the cold vault, on a lift that

399
00:32:08,346 --> 00:32:14,023
had always had four buttons. He loaded them into the
rack by hand — he did not trust the

400
00:32:14,043 --> 00:32:19,590
technicians with it, and it was not their competence
he distrusted — and he catalogued

401
00:32:19,610 --> 00:32:25,286
them himself, in his own handwriting, on a card index
he had bought in a stationery shop

402
00:32:25,306 --> 00:32:27,875
because paper does not log who reads it.

403
00:32:28,275 --> 00:32:33,643
Then he closed the vault door, and spun the wheel,
and stood in the corridor in the dark

404
00:32:33,663 --> 00:32:35,784
with the cold coming off the steel.

405
00:32:36,184 --> 00:32:42,155
Four thousand nine hundred Points. Thirteen people who
could not disobey. Nine rooms under

406
00:32:42,175 --> 00:32:46,615
a car park that had cost him more than
half of everything he owned.

407
00:32:47,134 --> 00:32:47,826
Good.

408
00:33:04,514 --> 00:33:10,346
On the Monday of his second week, Albert Wesker
called a meeting, and told nine people

409
00:33:10,365 --> 00:33:13,814
what the company they worked for
was going to sell.

410
00:33:14,215 --> 00:33:20,136
They had been expecting a strategy session. What they
got was closer to a weather report:

411
00:33:20,156 --> 00:33:25,745
brief, factual, and about a storm that had already
been decided upon somewhere else.

412
00:33:26,265 --> 00:33:34,167
We make diagnostics. Specifically, we make one: a
pathogen panel. Blood or swab in, forty-one

413
00:33:34,187 --> 00:33:41,833
organisms out, under twenty minutes, on a benchtop box
a clinic in Ohio can afford without

414
00:33:41,853 --> 00:33:42,514
a grant.

415
00:33:42,915 --> 00:33:49,235
A hand went up. One of Voss's juniors, twenty-six
years old, brave for four more seconds.

416
00:33:49,754 --> 00:33:52,944
Director — that's a crowded market.
Everyone has a panel.

417
00:33:53,346 --> 00:34:00,060
Everyone has a panel that takes ninety minutes,
costs four hundred dollars a run, and

418
00:34:00,080 --> 00:34:06,796
is wrong about coinfections. Ours will take
nineteen minutes, cost thirty-one dollars

419
00:34:06,817 --> 00:34:09,096
a run, and will not be wrong.

420
00:34:09,496 --> 00:34:10,625
How do you know?

421
00:34:11,025 --> 00:34:17,181
And Wesker looked at him, for rather longer than was
comfortable, with the flat patience

422
00:34:17,201 --> 00:34:20,480
of a man watching a dog work out
a door handle.

423
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:28,057
Because I have read the literature, Mr. Aldridge, and
because I have read what the literature

424
00:34:28,077 --> 00:34:30,569
was careful not to say. Sit down.

425
00:34:31,599 --> 00:34:39,586
Blueprints. Rapid Pathogen Assay, a forty-one target
panel: two thousand five hundred.

426
00:34:39,606 --> 00:34:47,780
Stable Protein Scaffold Library: two thousand two
hundred. Veterinary Antiviral: fifteen

427
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:48,525
hundred.

428
00:34:49,115 --> 00:34:50,065
Purchase.

429
00:34:51,096 --> 00:34:59,029
Purchase. Rapid Pathogen Assay. Two thousand five
hundred. Balance, two thousand four

430
00:34:59,049 --> 00:35:03,240
hundred. Delivered to Sublevel
B4, bench two.

431
00:35:03,829 --> 00:35:09,195
It did not appear in a flash of light. It appeared
the way everything appeared: as though

432
00:35:09,215 --> 00:35:10,949
it had been there for a year.

433
00:35:11,349 --> 00:35:17,875
Six binders of process documentation. Validation data
from two hundred and eleven clinical

434
00:35:17,895 --> 00:35:24,565
samples. A manufacturing specification down to the
grade of the plastic. A cartridge design.

435
00:35:24,585 --> 00:35:31,038
A reagent formulation. Three years of somebody's
meticulous work, in a lab that had never

436
00:35:31,058 --> 00:35:34,819
done it, signed off by scientists
who did not exist.

437
00:35:35,219 --> 00:35:40,012
Wesker read all of it in a night, which is
not a figure of speech.

438
00:35:40,413 --> 00:35:43,634
And then, because he was who he
was, he improved it.

439
00:35:44,033 --> 00:35:50,413
The System's design ran its reaction in twenty-two
minutes and called it twenty. The limiting

440
00:35:50,433 --> 00:35:56,677
step was the lysis: getting a virus or a bacterium
open fast enough to read what was inside

441
00:35:56,697 --> 00:35:59,844
it without destroying what you
wanted to read.

442
00:36:00,242 --> 00:36:06,572
The blueprint used heat and a chaotropic salt.
Everybody used heat and a chaotropic salt.

443
00:36:07,092 --> 00:36:15,793
And everybody has the same three failures. Enveloped
virus at low titre. Mycobacteria.

444
00:36:15,813 --> 00:36:16,502
Spores.

445
00:36:16,902 --> 00:36:22,753
He spent four nights on it, alone, with the vault open
and a rack of the archive's stranger

446
00:36:22,773 --> 00:36:28,300
cell lines thawing beside him, and on the fourth
night he cut the reaction to nineteen

447
00:36:28,320 --> 00:36:34,235
minutes by doing something that no competitor's chemist
would have dared put in a regulatory

448
00:36:34,255 --> 00:36:37,653
filing: he stopped trying to open
everything at once.

449
00:36:38,054 --> 00:36:43,940
Two chambers instead of one. A gentle enzymatic step
for the fragile organisms, running

450
00:36:43,960 --> 00:36:50,117
in parallel with a brutal mechanical one for the tough
ones, and the two streams recombined

451
00:36:50,137 --> 00:36:55,956
at the amplification stage with an internal control
that corrected for the difference.

452
00:36:55,975 --> 00:37:02,134
It was inelegant. It was, in the phrase Voss used when
she saw it, *offensively practical.*

453
00:37:02,153 --> 00:37:08,107
And it made the panel correct about the exact cases
that every other panel on the market

454
00:37:08,127 --> 00:37:12,384
got wrong: the patient with two
infections, one of them hiding.

455
00:37:12,904 --> 00:37:14,554
Who thinks like this?

456
00:37:14,953 --> 00:37:19,304
Someone who has been on the other end
of a false negative.

457
00:37:19,703 --> 00:37:25,292
He said it without any particular weight, and Dr.
Lena Voss did not ask what he meant,

458
00:37:25,313 --> 00:37:31,030
and the narrator will simply note that Albert Wesker
had once, in another world, watched

459
00:37:31,050 --> 00:37:36,769
a containment protocol fail because a test came back
clean on a Tuesday, and that he had

460
00:37:36,789 --> 00:37:41,333
learned from it, and that the thing he had
learned was not compassion.

461
00:37:42,484 --> 00:37:51,061
Patent filed. Two-stream lysis with cross-normalised
internal control. Inventor: A. Wesker.

462
00:37:51,081 --> 00:37:55,596
Acting with intelligence: twelve
hundred Points.

463
00:37:56,185 --> 00:38:00,596
Mr. Pell did the rest, and Mr. Pell, it
turned out, was a kind of weapon.

464
00:38:00,994 --> 00:38:06,725
He never raised his voice. He never appeared to
hurry. He arrived each morning at seven

465
00:38:06,746 --> 00:38:12,607
twelve with a folder, and over six weeks that folder
quietly turned a certified blueprint

466
00:38:12,627 --> 00:38:13,664
into a business.

467
00:38:14,185 --> 00:38:18,920
The device qualifies as a moderate-complexity panel.
There is a predicate on the market,

468
00:38:18,940 --> 00:38:23,567
which means clearance rather than approval: four
months, not four years. I have filed.

469
00:38:23,586 --> 00:38:28,375
The submission is complete and correct and there is
nothing in it for a reviewer to want.

470
00:38:28,775 --> 00:38:30,105
Manufacturing.

471
00:38:30,505 --> 00:38:35,435
Contracted in Puerto Rico for the first eighteen
months, at a price that is high because

472
00:38:35,456 --> 00:38:40,219
we are unknown. I have taken the high price and a
clause that lets us leave on thirty

473
00:38:40,239 --> 00:38:43,875
days' notice, because we will not be
unknown for eighteen months.

474
00:38:44,275 --> 00:38:45,784
Price to the customer.

475
00:38:46,185 --> 00:38:51,453
The market is three hundred and eighty dollars a run.
Our cost at volume is nine. I would

476
00:38:51,473 --> 00:38:53,295
suggest one hundred and ninety.

477
00:38:53,695 --> 00:38:54,565
Thirty-one.

478
00:38:54,965 --> 00:38:56,175
Pell's pen stopped.

479
00:38:56,695 --> 00:39:01,783
Director. At thirty-one dollars we make twenty-two
dollars a cartridge instead of a hundred

480
00:39:01,804 --> 00:39:02,625
and eighty-one.

481
00:39:03,025 --> 00:39:09,313
At thirty-one dollars every hospital network in the
mid-Atlantic replaces its instruments

482
00:39:09,333 --> 00:39:15,619
inside a year, and the instrument they replace them
with is ours, and the cartridges only

483
00:39:15,639 --> 00:39:21,784
fit ours, and in four years there is no such thing
as a three-hundred-and-eighty-dollar

484
00:39:21,804 --> 00:39:27,878
panel because I will have made the price of being
wrong about a patient too obvious to

485
00:39:27,898 --> 00:39:28,375
defend.

486
00:39:28,994 --> 00:39:30,965
(flat) Thirty-one dollars.

487
00:39:31,364 --> 00:39:39,594
And, Mr. Pell — at cost to any public health
laboratory that asks. Every one of them.

488
00:39:39,614 --> 00:39:44,154
No paperwork, no negotiation, no
press release.

489
00:39:44,554 --> 00:39:47,864
That is a considerable amount of
money to give away.

490
00:39:48,266 --> 00:39:54,266
It is a considerable amount of money to *lend*. They
will have our boxes on their benches

491
00:39:54,286 --> 00:40:00,286
when something frightening happens, and when
something frightening happens they will call

492
00:40:00,306 --> 00:40:01,775
the number on the box.

493
00:40:02,174 --> 00:40:08,125
The narrator notes, for the record, that the first
genuinely evil thing Albert Wesker

494
00:40:08,144 --> 00:40:14,375
did in this world was to give away a very good
medical test to the people who most needed

495
00:40:14,395 --> 00:40:17,326
it, for free, on purpose, ten years early.

496
00:40:17,724 --> 00:40:23,445
The clearance came through on the fourth of
November. The first orders came in on the

497
00:40:23,465 --> 00:40:29,456
ninth. On the twelfth, a man named Graham Tolliver
drove up from Trenton with his general

498
00:40:29,476 --> 00:40:35,399
counsel and asked for a meeting, and was given one at
four o'clock, and was kept waiting

499
00:40:35,420 --> 00:40:36,615
until four twenty.

500
00:40:37,016 --> 00:40:42,994
Tolliver was the chief executive of Verdant Axis
Therapeutics: thirteen hundred employees,

501
00:40:43,014 --> 00:40:49,059
a listed stock, and a syndromic panel that had taken
his company nine years and two hundred

502
00:40:49,079 --> 00:40:55,259
million dollars to build. It ran in ninety minutes and
cost three hundred and eighty dollars,

503
00:40:55,278 --> 00:40:59,724
and until the fourth of November it had
been the best in its class.

504
00:41:00,125 --> 00:41:05,451
He had come to buy the patent. He had a number in his
pocket and a threat behind it, and

505
00:41:05,471 --> 00:41:07,456
he had rehearsed both in the car.

506
00:41:07,855 --> 00:41:12,246
Wesker came in at four twenty,
alone, with no folder.

507
00:41:12,766 --> 00:41:14,735
Mr. Tolliver.

508
00:41:15,135 --> 00:41:17,045
You've kept me waiting twenty minutes.

509
00:41:17,445 --> 00:41:18,335
Yes.

510
00:41:18,734 --> 00:41:24,804
He sat down. He did not apologise and he did not
explain, and the silence that followed

511
00:41:24,824 --> 00:41:31,244
lasted long enough that Tolliver's counsel began the
pitch himself, out of sheer discomfort,

512
00:41:31,264 --> 00:41:33,904
four minutes before he had planned to.

513
00:41:34,425 --> 00:41:39,027
My client is prepared to acquire the two-stream lysis
patent and the associated instrument

514
00:41:39,047 --> 00:41:43,445
line for forty million dollars. To be frank, Dr.
Wesker, that is a generous number for

515
00:41:43,465 --> 00:41:48,119
a company that shipped its first unit nine days ago.
And I should say that we have reviewed

516
00:41:48,139 --> 00:41:52,538
your filings and we believe there are — overlaps —
with our own portfolio, which would

517
00:41:52,557 --> 00:41:55,054
be expensive for a firm of your
size to litigate.

518
00:41:55,454 --> 00:41:59,693
Wesker looked at him with what appeared
to be polite interest.

519
00:42:00,213 --> 00:42:01,323
Which claim?

520
00:42:01,724 --> 00:42:02,634
I'm sorry?

521
00:42:03,032 --> 00:42:09,947
You have asserted an overlap. Tell me which claim of
which patent. You have eight in this

522
00:42:09,967 --> 00:42:16,728
space. Nine of them are amplification chemistry,
which I do not use. One is a cartridge

523
00:42:16,748 --> 00:42:23,429
geometry that expired in March, and one is the
ninety-minute thermal protocol, which I

524
00:42:23,448 --> 00:42:26,623
would not want if you paid me to take it.

525
00:42:27,297 --> 00:42:33,001
The counsel opened his folder. Wesker had not
brought a folder, and the counsel's was

526
00:42:33,021 --> 00:42:35,628
suddenly the loudest thing in the room.

527
00:42:36,148 --> 00:42:42,579
You came here with a number and a bluff, and the
number is an insult and the bluff is

528
00:42:42,599 --> 00:42:49,183
a question you cannot answer in front of your own
chief executive. Let us not spend the

529
00:42:49,203 --> 00:42:50,398
afternoon on it.

530
00:42:50,797 --> 00:42:52,969
Then let's talk seriously. Eighty million.

531
00:42:53,368 --> 00:42:54,099
No.

532
00:42:54,498 --> 00:42:56,989
A hundred and twenty. That's more
than your entire —

533
00:42:57,389 --> 00:43:04,146
Mr. Tolliver. Your Q3 statement shows a hundred and
ninety million in panel revenue, which

534
00:43:04,166 --> 00:43:10,699
is sixty-one per cent of your business, and a debt
covenant that tests against trailing

535
00:43:10,719 --> 00:43:17,177
revenue every quarter. You have already lost the
Christiana network, and you will lose

536
00:43:17,197 --> 00:43:23,956
Jefferson within the month, because I have priced a
better test at eight per cent of yours

537
00:43:23,976 --> 00:43:30,509
and I have no shareholders to explain it to. By
March your trailing revenue will breach

538
00:43:30,529 --> 00:43:37,213
that covenant. Your lender will not renegotiate,
because your lender has spent four years

539
00:43:37,233 --> 00:43:43,916
looking for a reason not to. You did not come here to
buy a patent. You came here because

540
00:43:43,936 --> 00:43:47,757
you have a fuse and you have read
the length of it.

541
00:43:48,157 --> 00:43:49,349
Nobody spoke.

542
00:43:49,748 --> 00:43:55,091
The narrator would like to record what was actually
happening in that room, because it

543
00:43:55,112 --> 00:44:00,456
happened at every scale for the next sixty-five
years and the mechanism never changed.

544
00:44:00,475 --> 00:44:05,819
Wesker had not threatened anyone. He had not lied.
He had simply arrived in possession

545
00:44:05,840 --> 00:44:11,183
of every fact about the other man's life, arranged
them on the table in the order that

546
00:44:11,203 --> 00:44:16,298
hurt most, and then waited, without expression,
for the other man to look at them.

547
00:44:16,817 --> 00:44:20,947
I will not sell you the patent. I
will license it to you.

548
00:44:21,349 --> 00:44:22,297
License it.

549
00:44:22,697 --> 00:44:29,211
Non-exclusive, five years, for your instrument line.
It will let you cut your run time

550
00:44:29,231 --> 00:44:35,898
to thirty-one minutes and your price to sixty
dollars, which will save your covenant and

551
00:44:35,917 --> 00:44:42,355
your job. The fee is nine million a year, and four
per cent of the panel revenue, and

552
00:44:42,375 --> 00:44:43,648
one further term.

553
00:44:44,047 --> 00:44:44,877
Which is?

554
00:44:45,277 --> 00:44:52,746
Verdant Axis withdraws from the public-health tender in
Pennsylvania, New Jersey and Delaware.

555
00:44:52,765 --> 00:44:57,047
You will not bid against me for
government work again.

556
00:44:57,447 --> 00:45:02,998
The general counsel started to say something about
restraint of trade. Tolliver put a

557
00:45:03,018 --> 00:45:08,699
hand on his arm without looking at him — the gesture
of a man who has stopped listening

558
00:45:08,719 --> 00:45:14,532
to advice — and stared across the table at a face he
could not see behind a pair of black

559
00:45:14,552 --> 00:45:15,056
glasses.

560
00:45:15,576 --> 00:45:16,866
You're letting me live.

561
00:45:17,268 --> 00:45:22,974
I am letting you *work*. There is a difference, and
you will feel it every quarter when

562
00:45:22,994 --> 00:45:24,358
the payment goes out.

563
00:45:24,757 --> 00:45:31,018
He signed on the twenty-second of November. Verdant
Axis's stock went up eleven per cent

564
00:45:31,038 --> 00:45:37,369
on the announcement of the licence, and Graham
Tolliver was praised in two trade journals

565
00:45:37,389 --> 00:45:38,868
for his decisiveness.

566
00:45:40,018 --> 00:45:48,907
Subjugation of a rival. Verdant Axis Therapeutics:
licensed, constrained, retained. Fourteen

567
00:45:48,926 --> 00:45:55,587
thousand Points. Balance, seventeen
thousand six hundred. Level four.

568
00:45:56,176 --> 00:45:59,038
And the other one, quietly, from the
back of the skull.

569
00:45:59,556 --> 00:46:01,951
You should have taken the company.

570
00:46:02,349 --> 00:46:09,710
I have taken the company. I have merely left him in it
to run it for me, at his own expense,

571
00:46:09,730 --> 00:46:12,920
while he tells everyone it was his idea.

572
00:46:13,319 --> 00:46:16,953
That is not a victory anyone can see.

573
00:46:17,355 --> 00:46:18,143
Correct.

574
00:46:18,543 --> 00:46:22,873
By the middle of December, the boxes were
in ninety-one hospitals.

575
00:46:23,273 --> 00:46:28,666
A charge nurse in Chester told a reporter she had
found two infections in one child on

576
00:46:28,686 --> 00:46:34,268
a Sunday night with nobody senior in the building,
and that the old test would have found

577
00:46:34,288 --> 00:46:39,931
one. A public health laboratory in Dover ran four
hundred samples in a week on instruments

578
00:46:39,951 --> 00:46:45,658
it had not paid for. An infectious disease physician
in Baltimore wrote an editorial titled

579
00:46:45,679 --> 00:46:51,322
*The Thirty-One Dollar Question*, which was not about
Umbrella at all, and which mentioned

580
00:46:51,341 --> 00:46:52,203
Umbrella once.

581
00:46:52,603 --> 00:46:54,573
Nobody, anywhere, was frightened.

582
00:46:54,974 --> 00:47:00,433
On the last night of the year Pell came up with the
folder, as he did every night, and

583
00:47:00,453 --> 00:47:05,213
read the numbers aloud in the flat voice of a
man reciting a shopping list.

584
00:47:05,733 --> 00:47:10,559
Units placed: four hundred and six. Cartridges
shipped in December: two hundred and nine

585
00:47:10,579 --> 00:47:15,516
thousand. Licence income from Verdant Axis: nine
million, first instalment received. Cash:

586
00:47:15,536 --> 00:47:20,364
thirty-one million, four hundred thousand. Headcount:
thirty-eight. Total assets, on the

587
00:47:20,384 --> 00:47:23,283
balance sheet as it will be filed:
fifty-two million.

588
00:47:23,682 --> 00:47:29,134
Wesker had been standing at the window with his
back to the room. He turned around.

589
00:47:29,652 --> 00:47:34,264
Read that last figure again, Mr.
Pell. Slowly.

590
00:47:34,663 --> 00:47:36,893
(flat) Fifty-two million dollars.

591
00:47:37,922 --> 00:47:46,213
Main mission, tier two. A product worth selling:
complete. Fifty million dollars in assets:

592
00:47:46,233 --> 00:47:50,322
complete. Fifty staff:
thirty-eight of fifty.

593
00:47:50,913 --> 00:47:56,457
Twelve people short. Fifteen weeks from a chair in an
empty office to twelve people short

594
00:47:56,478 --> 00:47:57,083
of a tier.

595
00:47:57,483 --> 00:48:03,134
He looked at the panel for a moment with his hands
behind his back, and then he said the

596
00:48:03,153 --> 00:48:08,867
thing that the System, in all its billions of
candidates and all its criteria, had chosen

597
00:48:08,887 --> 00:48:09,384
him for.

598
00:48:09,902 --> 00:48:16,340
Then we will hire twelve people tomorrow, and the day
after that I would like to discuss

599
00:48:27,652 --> 00:48:33,759
Twelve people were hired in the first week of
January. Real people, with families and

600
00:48:33,779 --> 00:48:40,462
student loans and opinions: two process engineers, a
quality manager, a regulatory associate,

601
00:48:40,482 --> 00:48:46,588
four manufacturing technicians for the plant they
did not yet have, and four more for

602
00:48:46,608 --> 00:48:46,948
Voss.

603
00:48:47,348 --> 00:48:53,033
On the second of January the headcount passed fifty,
and the System put up a panel, and

604
00:48:53,053 --> 00:48:55,328
the panel was not what he expected.

605
00:48:56,478 --> 00:49:05,328
Main mission, tier two. A product worth selling:
complete. Fifty million in assets: complete.

606
00:49:05,348 --> 00:49:13,243
Fifty staff: complete. Hidden requirement, now
revealed: one rival absorbed entire.

607
00:49:13,835 --> 00:49:14,904
Explain.

608
00:49:15,304 --> 00:49:23,152
A company at tier two does not merely sell. It eats. You
have leased a rival his own survival.

609
00:49:23,172 --> 00:49:27,923
That is a tier one manoeuvre and it
was an excellent one.

610
00:49:28,324 --> 00:49:31,074
You did not disclose this requirement.

611
00:49:31,473 --> 00:49:36,364
You did not ask what tier two means.
You asked what it costs.

612
00:49:36,764 --> 00:49:43,217
There was a pause of perhaps four seconds, which in
the private history of Albert Wesker's

613
00:49:43,237 --> 00:49:46,453
relationship with the System
was a long time.

614
00:49:46,973 --> 00:49:51,063
You are going to do that to me for
sixty years, aren't you.

615
00:49:51,464 --> 00:49:52,274
Yes.

616
00:49:52,673 --> 00:49:55,923
Good. I would think less of you otherwise.

617
00:49:56,324 --> 00:50:00,514
And then he said the thing he had been turning
over since the twelfth of November.

618
00:50:01,033 --> 00:50:07,563
The Gacha. Explain the mathematics. Not the
theatre. The mathematics.

619
00:50:08,594 --> 00:50:16,760
The Company pool. Single pull, one thousand.
Ten-pull, nine thousand, with one Rare or

620
00:50:16,780 --> 00:50:25,043
better guaranteed. Common, sixty-two per cent.
Uncommon, twenty-seven. Rare, nine point

621
00:50:25,063 --> 00:50:33,134
four. Epic, one point four. Legendary, one in five
hundred. A Legendary is a package.

622
00:50:33,154 --> 00:50:36,180
Odds are not improved by belief.

623
00:50:36,771 --> 00:50:41,995
He read the table twice, and the narrator will tell
you what he was doing, because it

624
00:50:42,015 --> 00:50:45,820
was not what any other man in the world
would have been doing.

625
00:50:46,221 --> 00:50:51,746
He was not calculating whether to pull. He was
calculating what the numbers implied about

626
00:50:51,766 --> 00:50:57,230
the thing offering them. Sixty-two per cent junk. One
in five hundred for something that

627
00:50:57,250 --> 00:51:02,837
could change his year. Those were not the odds of a
machine that wanted to help him. Those

628
00:51:02,857 --> 00:51:08,259
were the odds of a machine that wanted him to *keep
pulling* — and a machine that wants

629
00:51:08,278 --> 00:51:12,371
something can be read, and a machine that
can be read can be used.

630
00:51:12,771 --> 00:51:15,820
The passenger had been
listening. It spoke now.

631
00:51:16,340 --> 00:51:17,898
It is a lottery.

632
00:51:18,298 --> 00:51:24,634
It is a lottery run by an entity that requires me
to grow in order to justify its own

633
00:51:24,654 --> 00:51:29,868
existence. Which makes it a lottery that
cannot afford to bankrupt me.

634
00:51:30,268 --> 00:51:33,011
Fate does not audit itself.

635
00:51:33,411 --> 00:51:36,821
Then it is fortunate for both of
us that I do.

636
00:51:37,221 --> 00:51:41,351
He spent nine thousand of his seventeen
thousand six hundred Points.

637
00:51:42,502 --> 00:51:44,131
Ten-pull. Resolving.

638
00:51:44,721 --> 00:51:50,641
It took ninety seconds, and it was designed —
deliberately, shamelessly — to be enjoyable.

639
00:51:51,041 --> 00:51:57,500
Grey light. *Common: Laboratory Technician.* Grey
