worse than being laughed at, because that was the
moment Marcus Reese understood that

1064
01:26:06,590 --> 01:26:11,968
being right and being believed were going to be two
different things for the rest of his

1065
01:26:11,988 --> 01:26:12,275
life.

1066
01:26:12,675 --> 01:26:19,081
He went quiet for four years. He comes back in 2031,
and by then he is very good at this,

1067
01:26:19,101 --> 01:26:21,825
and by then it does not matter at all.

1068
01:26:22,225 --> 01:26:28,471
On Monday morning, four hundred and six people —
everyone from Division Seven and everyone

1069
01:26:28,491 --> 01:26:34,945
from the Newark plant, bussed down at company expense —
sat in a five-hundred-seat auditorium

1070
01:26:34,965 --> 01:26:40,655
that had not existed on Friday, and waited for the
Director to explain the campus.

1071
01:26:41,055 --> 01:26:47,124
Wesker stood backstage in the dark with his hands
behind his back, and did something he

1072
01:26:47,144 --> 01:26:51,884
had not done since the volcano, and would
not do again for a decade.

1073
01:26:52,285 --> 01:26:53,515
He asked for help.

1074
01:26:54,035 --> 01:26:55,765
Say something useful.

1075
01:26:56,165 --> 01:27:00,060
You will walk out there and tell
them the facts.

1076
01:27:00,459 --> 01:27:06,300
I will tell them the truth, which is that we are the
fastest-growing diagnostics company

1077
01:27:06,320 --> 01:27:10,430
in North America and that there is a
great deal of work to do.

1078
01:27:10,829 --> 01:27:17,479
And they will clap, and they will go back to their
benches, and by Thursday it will be

1079
01:27:17,499 --> 01:27:23,528
as though you had sent an email. Listen to me.
What do they feel this morning?

1080
01:27:23,929 --> 01:27:31,606
Frightened. The plant workers think the Wilmington
staff look down on them. The Wilmington

1081
01:27:31,626 --> 01:27:39,218
staff think the plant means the science is over.
Everyone in that room saw four buildings

1082
01:27:39,238 --> 01:27:44,179
appear over a weekend and has decided
not to ask about it.

1083
01:27:44,579 --> 01:27:46,789
Then do not reassure them.

1084
01:27:47,189 --> 01:27:48,039
Go on.

1085
01:27:48,439 --> 01:27:55,742
Reassurance is what a manager gives. Give them the
fear itself, out loud, in your own

1086
01:27:55,762 --> 01:28:03,928
mouth, before any of them dares say it. A crowd will
forgive you for anything except pretending

1087
01:28:03,948 --> 01:28:11,510
that the thing in the room is not in the room. Name
it. Then stand still and say nothing

1088
01:28:11,530 --> 01:28:19,179
for as long as you can bear it. Then give them
something enormous to be afraid of instead

1089
01:28:19,199 --> 01:28:22,194
— something that is not themselves.

1090
01:28:22,594 --> 01:28:23,515
A pause.

1091
01:28:24,035 --> 01:28:25,972
And the pause is how long?

1092
01:28:26,372 --> 01:28:30,725
Until it hurts. Then two seconds more.

1093
01:28:31,125 --> 01:28:37,115
He walked out. No slides. No notes. No introduction
— he had told Catherine to cut it.

1094
01:28:37,635 --> 01:28:42,105
There are four buildings outside that
were not there on Friday.

1095
01:28:43,275 --> 01:28:45,965
Four hundred and six people
stopped moving.

1096
01:28:46,484 --> 01:28:53,183
Nobody in this room is going to ask me how. I know
that, because it is Monday and not

1097
01:28:53,203 --> 01:29:00,060
one of you has asked. You have all decided,
privately, to be adults about it, and to be

1098
01:29:00,080 --> 01:29:05,434
grateful, and to go back to work. I want
you to know that I noticed.

1099
01:29:06,155 --> 01:29:11,944
I am not going to explain it to you today. I am
going to tell you what it is for.

1100
01:29:12,344 --> 01:29:14,584
And then he gave them the enormous thing.

1101
01:29:15,104 --> 01:29:22,194
Four hundred million people on this planet will be
infected this year with something that

1102
01:29:22,214 --> 01:29:29,543
a nineteen-dollar test could have caught in nineteen
minutes. Roughly one and a half million

1103
01:29:29,563 --> 01:29:37,052
of them will die of it — not of anything exotic, not of
anything incurable, but of a diagnosis

1104
01:29:37,072 --> 01:29:43,842
that arrived three days late. Every one of those
deaths is an *administrative* death.

1105
01:29:43,862 --> 01:29:48,714
Somebody, somewhere, decided that the
test was too expensive.

1106
01:29:48,994 --> 01:29:55,915
I have made it thirty-one dollars. I will make it
fourteen. I intend to make it free,

1107
01:29:55,935 --> 01:30:02,856
everywhere, and I intend for that to be the least
interesting thing this company ever

1108
01:30:02,876 --> 01:30:03,264
does.

1109
01:30:03,545 --> 01:30:09,748
You are not going to be thanked for it. There will
be no parade. In ten years, people

1110
01:30:09,768 --> 01:30:15,972
will use what you built here the way they use tap
water, and they will complain about

1111
01:30:15,992 --> 01:30:18,755
it, and they will not know your names.

1112
01:30:19,364 --> 01:30:26,095
I will know your names. I know all of them now. That
is the arrangement I am offering,

1113
01:30:26,115 --> 01:30:30,334
and it is the only one I have ever
found worth making.

1114
01:30:30,734 --> 01:30:36,053
He stopped talking. He did not say thank you, and he
did not wait to be applauded, and

1115
01:30:36,073 --> 01:30:39,964
he was three steps towards the wing
before the room came apart.

1116
01:30:40,364 --> 01:30:45,780
They stood. All of them, in about a second and a
half, including three hundred and eleven

1117
01:30:45,800 --> 01:30:51,094
people who had been alive for nine weeks and
fifty-one who had not, and a British chief

1118
01:30:51,114 --> 01:30:56,651
financial officer at the back who found that she had
got to her feet without having decided

1119
01:30:56,671 --> 01:30:56,834
to.

1120
01:30:57,984 --> 01:31:06,682
Devotion. Four hundred and six personnel addressed.
Morale: transformed. Thirty-four thousand

1121
01:31:06,702 --> 01:31:15,025
four hundred Points. Level ten. Note: the passenger's
craft is operationally significant.

1122
01:31:15,045 --> 01:31:17,275
Recommend continued use.

1123
01:31:17,864 --> 01:31:24,300
In the wing, in the dark, Albert Wesker stood with
his back against a wall and read that

1124
01:31:24,320 --> 01:31:27,674
panel twice, and what he felt
was not triumph.

1125
01:31:28,194 --> 01:31:30,425
*Recommend continued use.*

1126
01:31:30,824 --> 01:31:32,120
You are welcome.

1127
01:31:32,520 --> 01:31:35,330
I had them in twenty-one sentences.

1128
01:31:35,729 --> 01:31:42,403
You had them in the *pause*. You would have talked
through it. You have talked through

1129
01:31:42,423 --> 01:31:49,485
every silence you have ever been given, because
silence is the one room you cannot control.

1130
01:31:50,161 --> 01:31:51,031
Noted.

1131
01:31:51,431 --> 01:31:56,519
And the narrator would like to point out, because
nobody in that building could have,

1132
01:31:56,539 --> 01:32:01,867
that this was the single most dangerous minute in the
first year of Umbrella's existence.

1133
01:32:01,887 --> 01:32:07,215
Not the Tyrant. Not the plant. This: the moment the
man who could not be moved discovered

1134
01:32:07,235 --> 01:32:12,383
that the passenger in his skull was carrying the one
tool he did not have, and decided

1135
01:32:12,403 --> 01:32:17,551
— coldly, without pleasure, the way he decided
everything — that he was going to start

1136
01:32:17,571 --> 01:32:18,092
using it.

1137
01:32:18,492 --> 01:32:22,622
Catherine found him on the roof that night
with a drawing of the campus.

1138
01:32:23,142 --> 01:32:24,588
Four hundred Points.

1139
01:32:24,988 --> 01:32:26,559
I beg your pardon?

1140
01:32:26,958 --> 01:32:31,693
That's what you said to Pell on the phone. "Four hundred
left." He assumed you meant thousand.

1141
01:32:31,713 --> 01:32:32,805
I don't think you did.

1142
01:32:33,205 --> 01:32:38,235
Wesker looked out at five buildings that had
cost him everything he had.

1143
01:32:38,756 --> 01:32:40,766
No. I did not.

1144
01:32:41,165 --> 01:32:45,553
I've stopped asking where the money comes from. I
want you to know that's a decision I

1145
01:32:45,573 --> 01:32:47,449
made, not a thing I failed to notice.

1146
01:32:47,849 --> 01:32:48,660
I know.

1147
01:32:49,059 --> 01:32:51,280
I want to know what you're going
to do with it.

1148
01:32:51,680 --> 01:32:57,626
And he told her — not all of it, not the volcano or
the merge or the panel that hung in

1149
01:32:57,646 --> 01:33:03,455
the air where nobody else could see it, but the
shape of the next ten years, in about

1150
01:33:03,475 --> 01:33:08,393
ninety seconds, in the flat tone of a man
reading out a train timetable.

1151
01:33:08,792 --> 01:33:14,787
When he had finished, Catherine Adeyemi stood on the
roof of a building that had appeared

1152
01:33:14,807 --> 01:33:20,599
out of nothing, and heard a man describe a company
larger than any state on earth, and

1153
01:33:20,619 --> 01:33:26,344
believed every word, and understood in that moment
that she had already chosen, weeks

1154
01:33:26,364 --> 01:33:29,183
ago, in a boardroom, over a cup of coffee.

1155
01:33:29,703 --> 01:33:30,899
That's monstrous.

1156
01:33:31,299 --> 01:33:33,729
Yes. Is it wrong?

1157
01:33:34,128 --> 01:33:38,579
She thought about it for a long time. That is
the detail. She thought about it.

1158
01:33:39,098 --> 01:33:40,689
Ask me in five years.

1159
01:33:53,038 --> 01:33:58,249
Graham Tolliver had saved his company, and
everybody had told him so.

1160
01:33:58,649 --> 01:34:03,954
The licence had cut his run time to thirty-one
minutes. The trade press had called him

1161
01:34:03,974 --> 01:34:09,650
decisive. His lender had stopped calling on Fridays.
And every quarter, nine million dollars

1162
01:34:09,670 --> 01:34:15,037
left his accounts and went to Wilmington, and four
per cent of every panel he sold went

1163
01:34:15,057 --> 01:34:20,425
with it, and at the end of the first quarter his
chief financial officer put a slide in

1164
01:34:20,445 --> 01:34:25,750
front of him showing the five-year projection, which
was a straight line going down at

1165
01:34:25,770 --> 01:34:26,678
a polite angle.

1166
01:34:27,079 --> 01:34:31,379
It is one thing to lose. It is another
thing entirely to be kept.

1167
01:34:31,778 --> 01:34:37,862
On the sixth of April, Tolliver stopped negotiating
with Albert Wesker and started fighting

1168
01:34:37,882 --> 01:34:43,563
him, and the narrator would like to say, before any
of this begins, that he was not a

1169
01:34:43,583 --> 01:34:49,332
fool. He was a competent chief executive with eleven
hundred employees who depended on

1170
01:34:49,352 --> 01:34:53,758
him, and every single thing he tried was
the correct thing to try.

1171
01:34:54,159 --> 01:34:56,549
He tried three moves in eleven days.

1172
01:34:56,948 --> 01:35:03,102
The first was legal: a suit in the District of
Delaware alleging that the archive licence

1173
01:35:03,122 --> 01:35:09,207
Verdant Axis had bought in 2019 had been exclusive in
substance if not in form, and that

1174
01:35:09,227 --> 01:35:15,381
Umbrella's entire instrument line was built on
material it had no right to commercialise.

1175
01:35:15,401 --> 01:35:21,347
It was a stretch. It was also the kind of stretch
that takes two years and twenty-nine

1176
01:35:21,367 --> 01:35:23,358
million dollars to unstretch.

1177
01:35:23,758 --> 01:35:30,067
The second was people: offers to Evelyn Marsh and
to two of Voss's process engineers,

1178
01:35:30,087 --> 01:35:35,429
at two and a half times their salaries, with
signing bonuses and titles.

1179
01:35:35,829 --> 01:35:41,625
The third was quieter. He hired a firm in Virginia —
the kind with a two-word name and

1180
01:35:41,645 --> 01:35:47,576
no website — to find out what was in the building in
Wilmington. What he said, on a call

1181
01:35:47,596 --> 01:35:53,662
that was later read into evidence in a hearing that
went nowhere, was: *I don't want dirt.

1182
01:35:53,682 --> 01:35:59,479
I want to know how they did it in ninety days.
Because nobody does it in ninety days.*

1183
01:35:59,878 --> 01:36:02,908
And that, of the three, was the
one that ended him.

1184
01:36:03,308 --> 01:36:05,558
Wesker did not defend.

1185
01:36:05,958 --> 01:36:12,169
This is the thing that everyone who ever fought him got
wrong, at every scale, for sixty-five

1186
01:36:12,189 --> 01:36:18,400
years, right up to a hive at the edge of a dead system:
they expected a defence, and prepared

1187
01:36:18,420 --> 01:36:24,296
for a defence, and what arrived instead was a man who
had already decided that the fight

1188
01:36:24,316 --> 01:36:26,909
was a distraction from the acquisition.

1189
01:36:27,428 --> 01:36:32,203
(flat) The complaint is served. Our counsel believes
we would win at summary judgment,

1190
01:36:32,223 --> 01:36:34,098
but not before March of next year.

1191
01:36:34,498 --> 01:36:38,508
Don't answer it yet. What does
their paper look like?

1192
01:36:38,908 --> 01:36:43,589
Two hundred and twenty-nine million in senior debt
held by a syndicate. Lead is Merrick

1193
01:36:43,609 --> 01:36:48,182
Trust — a regional lender, conservative, and
unhappy. They wrote the facility in 2023

1194
01:36:48,202 --> 01:36:52,883
when the panel was still the best in the market.
There is a covenant tested on trailing

1195
01:36:52,903 --> 01:36:57,799
twelve-month revenue, and a change-of-control clause,
and a provision permitting assignment

1196
01:36:57,819 --> 01:36:59,798
of the debt without borrower consent.

1197
01:37:00,199 --> 01:37:04,989
They may sell his loan to anyone at all,
and he cannot stop them.

1198
01:37:05,389 --> 01:37:06,359
They may.

1199
01:37:06,759 --> 01:37:08,009
What would it take?

1200
01:37:08,408 --> 01:37:13,142
The syndicate would part with the paper at
ninety-one cents. A hundred and ninety-two

1201
01:37:13,162 --> 01:37:15,658
million dollars. We have
thirty-nine in cash.

1202
01:37:16,058 --> 01:37:19,088
Wesker looked at the number for a moment.

1203
01:37:19,609 --> 01:37:20,519
Catherine.

1204
01:37:20,919 --> 01:37:27,665
Catherine Adeyemi spent six days on the telephone
and did what nine years in London had

1205
01:37:27,685 --> 01:37:28,365
been for.

1206
01:37:28,765 --> 01:37:34,153
She did not raise a hundred and ninety-two million
dollars. She raised thirty — a bridge

1207
01:37:34,173 --> 01:37:39,623
from a Boston credit fund against Umbrella's own
receivables and the Verdant Axis licence

1208
01:37:39,643 --> 01:37:44,908
stream, which was the single most reliable cash flow
in the industry precisely because

1209
01:37:44,928 --> 01:37:50,439
Tolliver could not stop paying it. Then she took the
thirty and bought the *lead* position

1210
01:37:50,459 --> 01:37:55,969
in the syndicate: not the whole loan, but the seat at
the table, with the amendment rights

1211
01:37:55,989 --> 01:37:57,075
that came with it.

1212
01:37:57,595 --> 01:38:01,847
I want to be clear about what I've done, because when
this is over I will have to explain

1213
01:38:01,867 --> 01:38:06,022
it to an auditor. We have not bought his company. We
have bought the right to be in the

1214
01:38:06,042 --> 01:38:07,701
room when his company is discussed.

1215
01:38:08,101 --> 01:38:10,291
That is all I have ever needed.

1216
01:38:10,691 --> 01:38:15,154
There's something else. I met the Merrick people
twice. The second time, their credit

1217
01:38:15,174 --> 01:38:19,899
officer asked me — carefully, the way you ask a
question you've been told to ask — whether

1218
01:38:19,919 --> 01:38:24,645
Umbrella had any interest in acquiring Verdant Axis
outright. It was not an idle question.

1219
01:38:24,665 --> 01:38:29,391
Someone at that bank has already run the numbers on
you as an owner and liked them better.

1220
01:38:29,791 --> 01:38:36,220
Wesker was quiet for a moment, and the narrator would
like to note that this is the closest

1221
01:38:36,240 --> 01:38:41,961
anybody came, in the whole of that first year, to
catching the machinery at work.

1222
01:38:42,481 --> 01:38:44,011
Did they say why?

1223
01:38:44,411 --> 01:38:46,214
They said your paper is clean.

1224
01:38:46,614 --> 01:38:48,884
Yes. It is.

1225
01:38:49,284 --> 01:38:52,764
On the night of the thirteenth, four
men came over the wall.

1226
01:38:53,164 --> 01:38:58,502
They were not criminals, particularly. Two were
ex-military, one was a former Delaware

1227
01:38:58,522 --> 01:39:03,861
state trooper, and the fourth carried a camera with
a low-light sensor worth more than

1228
01:39:03,881 --> 01:39:09,345
the car they had arrived in. Their instructions were
to photograph the loading dock, the

1229
01:39:09,365 --> 01:39:14,766
transformer yard, and anything on the south side of
the building that looked newer than

1230
01:39:14,786 --> 01:39:16,574
the paperwork said it should.

1231
01:39:16,974 --> 01:39:22,154
They came over the wall at one fifty. They were
on the ground for eleven seconds.

1232
01:39:22,553 --> 01:39:28,192
Hale was standing on the service road when they
landed. Not running towards it — standing

1233
01:39:28,212 --> 01:39:33,914
on it, with his hands at his sides, having been in
that exact spot since one thirty-eight.

1234
01:39:34,433 --> 01:39:38,324
(flat) This is private property. You will
leave the way you came.

1235
01:39:38,724 --> 01:39:44,856
The man with the camera — his name was Wendell Tarr,
and he is the only one of the four

1236
01:39:44,876 --> 01:39:51,292
who ever spoke about it on the record — later
described what happened next in a deposition,

1237
01:39:51,312 --> 01:39:55,394
and then in a psychiatrist's office,
and then never again.

1238
01:39:55,794 --> 01:40:01,979
There was a man at the far end of the road, about
forty metres away, in a long black coat.

1239
01:40:01,999 --> 01:40:04,944
Tarr saw him clearly. He
raised the camera.

1240
01:40:05,344 --> 01:40:11,228
Between the moment the shutter opened and the moment
it closed — which, at the setting

1241
01:40:11,248 --> 01:40:17,270
he was using, was one one-hundred-and-sixtieth of a
second — the man was no longer forty

1242
01:40:17,290 --> 01:40:18,094
metres away.

1243
01:40:18,493 --> 01:40:24,216
He was standing beside Wendell Tarr, close enough to
touch, with one gloved hand resting

1244
01:40:24,236 --> 01:40:29,763
on the top of the camera, looking at him through a
pair of black glasses in the dark.

1245
01:40:30,669 --> 01:40:34,419
That is an excellent lens. What is it for?

1246
01:40:34,818 --> 01:40:40,343
Tarr could not answer. One of the others — the
trooper — went for something at his waist,

1247
01:40:40,363 --> 01:40:45,637
and Hale's hand closed over his wrist, and that was
the whole of the violence: a hand

1248
01:40:45,657 --> 01:40:47,568
on a wrist, which stayed there.

1249
01:40:48,089 --> 01:40:53,079
I am going to tell you four things, and then
you are going to go home.

1250
01:40:53,359 --> 01:40:59,813
The first is that nobody here is going to hurt you.
The second is that there is nothing

1251
01:40:59,833 --> 01:41:06,213
on this campus that would interest your client, and
he knows it, or he would have sent

1252
01:41:06,233 --> 01:41:12,762
people he was willing to lose. The third is that Mr.
Tolliver is paying you a great deal

1253
01:41:12,782 --> 01:41:19,162
of money out of a company that will not exist in
ninety days, so I would encourage you

1254
01:41:19,182 --> 01:41:20,949
to invoice him promptly.

1255
01:41:21,349 --> 01:41:23,359
He lifted his hand off the camera.

1256
01:41:23,878 --> 01:41:30,529
And the fourth is that you will find, when you try
to describe this, that the sentence

1257
01:41:30,549 --> 01:41:37,122
does not survive being spoken aloud. Try it in the
car. You will get about half of it

1258
01:41:37,142 --> 01:41:42,628
out, and then you will hear yourself, and
you will stop. Everyone does.

1259
01:41:43,029 --> 01:41:48,657
They went back over the wall. The memory card was not
taken from them — that is the part

1260
01:41:48,677 --> 01:41:54,240
that stayed with Tarr the longest. Nobody took the
card. They got to the car and looked

1261
01:41:54,260 --> 01:41:59,824
at the frame, and the frame showed an empty road
with a smear of black at the left-hand

1262
01:41:59,844 --> 01:42:03,419
edge, which any competent person
would call motion blur.

1263
01:42:03,818 --> 01:42:08,969
Three of the four quit the firm inside a month.
Wendell Tarr kept the photograph.

1264
01:42:10,118 --> 01:42:18,967
Acting with intelligence. Intrusion resolved without
injury, arrest or record. Four witnesses.

1265
01:42:18,987 --> 01:42:23,967
Zero credible accounts produced.
Six thousand Points.

1266
01:42:24,557 --> 01:42:27,947
From somewhere behind his eyes, the
passenger spoke.

1267
01:42:28,468 --> 01:42:30,222
You let them go.

1268
01:42:30,622 --> 01:42:37,388
I let them *carry* it. Four men who cannot repeat
what they saw, working in an industry

1269
01:42:37,407 --> 01:42:44,173
of three hundred people who all drink together. By
June every private intelligence firm

1270
01:42:44,193 --> 01:42:51,115
on this coast will have heard a story they do not
believe about a building in Wilmington,

1271
01:42:51,135 --> 01:42:56,731
and every one of them will quote a price for
it that no client will pay.

1272
01:42:57,132 --> 01:42:59,942
You have made yourself a rumour.

1273
01:43:00,341 --> 01:43:07,131
I have made myself an *expensive* rumour. It is the
cheapest security money can buy.

1274
01:43:07,532 --> 01:43:12,718
The board met at two o'clock on the fourth of May to
consider a proposal from its lead

1275
01:43:12,738 --> 01:43:13,142
lender.

1276
01:43:13,541 --> 01:43:19,490
The proposal was simple. Merrick Trust and the
syndicate would support a recapitalisation

1277
01:43:19,510 --> 01:43:25,257
in which Umbrella Life Sciences would acquire
Verdant Axis Therapeutics for a dollar a

1278
01:43:25,277 --> 01:43:31,025
share, assume the debt, retain the workforce, and
drop the litigation. The alternative

1279
01:43:31,045 --> 01:43:35,250
was to test the covenant in July, which
the company would fail.

1280
01:43:35,649 --> 01:43:41,593
Nine directors. They had been arguing for fifty
minutes when the door at the end of the

1281
01:43:41,613 --> 01:43:47,626
room opened and Wesker came in alone, twenty minutes
after he had been due, and sat down

1282
01:43:47,646 --> 01:43:50,300
in the empty chair without apologising.

1283
01:43:50,700 --> 01:43:56,300
He let them finish. That is the thing that the two
surviving directors both mentioned,

1284
01:43:56,320 --> 01:44:02,051
independently, years apart, to two different people.
He let them finish arguing, for six

1285
01:44:02,071 --> 01:44:05,450
more minutes, about a company he
had already bought.

1286
01:44:05,969 --> 01:44:07,780
You've had this planned since November.

1287
01:44:08,180 --> 01:44:11,290
Since the twelfth of November, yes.

1288
01:44:11,690 --> 01:44:15,180
You gave me a licence so I'd survive long
enough to be worth taking.

1289
01:44:15,580 --> 01:44:22,007
I gave you a licence so that you would spend six
months proving, in public, with audited

1290
01:44:22,027 --> 01:44:28,528
numbers, that my chemistry is worth nine million a
quarter to a competitor. You have been

1291
01:44:28,548 --> 01:44:34,610
my best salesman since Christmas. It would have
been discourteous to interrupt you.

1292
01:44:35,009 --> 01:44:40,721
Tolliver stood up. He was not a small man, and he
had been a serious person for thirty

1293
01:44:40,741 --> 01:44:46,586
years, and the narrator would like to record that
what he said next was the truest thing

1294
01:44:46,606 --> 01:44:48,253
anyone said in that room.

1295
01:44:48,772 --> 01:44:52,873
What *are* you? Nobody moves like this. Nobody
builds a plant in a night — I have the

1296
01:44:52,893 --> 01:44:57,140
aerial photographs, I have *satellite*, I have a
picture of that lot from 2023 and there

1297
01:44:57,160 --> 01:45:00,921
is a plant in it and I stood on that lot in 2023
and there was nothing there —

1298
01:45:01,321 --> 01:45:04,622
And Albert Wesker took off the sunglasses.

1299
01:45:05,571 --> 01:45:11,480
There is no record of what Graham Tolliver saw. The
minutes note only that the chief executive

1300
01:45:11,500 --> 01:45:16,841
sat down. The two directors who spoke about it
afterwards both said the same thing in

1301
01:45:16,861 --> 01:45:22,201
slightly different words: that the room went very
quiet, and that Tolliver's hand was

1302
01:45:22,221 --> 01:45:27,562
shaking against the table, and that neither of them
looked directly at the man in the

1303
01:45:27,582 --> 01:45:30,841
chair for the remaining four
minutes of the meeting.

1304
01:45:31,242 --> 01:45:33,072
The vote was nine to nothing.

1305
01:45:33,471 --> 01:45:36,231
He put the glasses back on
before he stood up.

1306
01:45:36,752 --> 01:45:43,098
Mr. Tolliver. Your contract has twelve months to
run and I intend to honour it. There

1307
01:45:43,118 --> 01:45:49,764
is an office for you in Trenton and a title with the
word *emeritus* in it. You will keep

1308
01:45:49,784 --> 01:45:56,429
your health plan and your pension and your parking
space, and you will never make another

1309
01:45:56,449 --> 01:45:58,601
decision as long as you live.

1310
01:45:59,002 --> 01:45:59,692
Why?

1311
01:46:00,091 --> 01:46:06,392
Because you fought me competently, and because in four
months, when people ask what happens

1312
01:46:06,412 --> 01:46:12,295
to men who fight me, I would like the answer to be
*nothing interesting*. It is a far

1313
01:46:12,315 --> 01:46:15,282
more frightening answer than
the other one.

1314
01:46:16,311 --> 01:46:24,644
Rival absorbed entire. Verdant Axis Therapeutics,
acquired. One thousand one hundred and

1315
01:46:24,664 --> 01:46:32,806
eight staff. Three facilities. Sixty-one patents.
Forty thousand Points. Level twelve.

1316
01:46:34,027 --> 01:46:41,640
Main mission, tier two: all requirements complete.
Tier advancement will resolve at the

1317
01:46:41,660 --> 01:46:43,308
next reward window.

1318
01:46:43,897 --> 01:46:49,906
The sign came down the same night. Wesker had
specified it: not in the morning, when the

1319
01:46:49,926 --> 01:46:55,934
staff would gather and photograph it and feel
something, but at half past nine at night,

1320
01:46:55,954 --> 01:46:58,948
with two men and a lift and
nobody watching.

1321
01:46:59,348 --> 01:47:05,083
In the morning there was simply a crimson iris on a
building in Trenton, and eleven hundred

1322
01:47:05,103 --> 01:47:10,522
people arriving at work for a company whose name had
changed while they slept, which —

1323
01:47:10,542 --> 01:47:16,025
by the standards of what had happened to the three
hundred and thirteen in Newark — was

1324
01:47:16,045 --> 01:47:18,808
an unusually gentle thing to do
to a person.

1325
01:47:19,208 --> 01:47:21,998
He stood in the car park for a
while afterwards.

1326
01:47:22,397 --> 01:47:25,568
The voice in his skull chose that
moment to arrive.

1327
01:47:26,088 --> 01:47:28,028
Eleven hundred more.

1328
01:47:28,429 --> 01:47:35,482
Eleven hundred who can quit, complain, sue, or talk
to a journalist. It is the largest

1329
01:47:35,502 --> 01:47:39,019
liability I have ever
voluntarily acquired.

1330
01:47:39,418 --> 01:47:41,496
And yet you are pleased.

1331
01:47:41,897 --> 01:47:47,996
I am pleased because eight months ago I was a man in
a chair with nine thousand Points

1332
01:47:48,016 --> 01:47:54,613
and a laboratory full of nothing, and tomorrow morning
I will own the second-best diagnostics

1333
01:47:54,633 --> 01:47:57,887
company on this continent as
well as the best.

1334
01:47:58,287 --> 01:48:00,357
He looked up at the iris on the wall.

1335
01:48:00,877 --> 01:48:07,052
And because tomorrow the System pays me for it, and
I have decided what I am going to

1336
01:48:19,257 --> 01:48:27,159
Tier two. Company. Umbrella Life Sciences,
Incorporated. Twenty-five thousand Points.

1337
01:48:27,179 --> 01:48:35,081
Balance, sixty-two thousand. Territory tab
unlocked. The Company banner is available.

1338
01:48:35,101 --> 01:48:39,276
Two hundred and eleven new
items in the Shop.

1339
01:48:39,865 --> 01:48:45,650
Eight months. From a chair in an empty office to the
second tier of six, in eight months,

1340
01:48:45,670 --> 01:48:51,389
with a balance of sixty-two thousand Points and a
company that employed one thousand six

1341
01:48:51,409 --> 01:48:57,062
hundred and eleven people, three hundred and
twenty-three of whom had been manufactured

1342
01:48:57,082 --> 01:48:57,975
on a Saturday.

1343
01:48:58,375 --> 01:49:04,117
He read the new tabs for two hours. Territory, which
was locked at a price he could not

1344
01:49:04,137 --> 01:49:10,012
yet reach and which made his hands very still when he
read what it contained. Facilities,

1345
01:49:10,032 --> 01:49:15,973
which now offered things with words like *secure
estate* and *subsurface complex* in them.

1346
01:49:15,993 --> 01:49:20,012
Personnel, which now offered cohorts
rather than individuals.

1347
01:49:20,413 --> 01:49:21,753
And the Company banner.

1348
01:49:22,904 --> 01:49:31,227
The Company banner. Ten-pull, twenty thousand, with one
Epic or better guaranteed. Legendary

1349
01:49:31,247 --> 01:49:36,578
pool at this tier: packages. A place,
and everything in it.

1350
01:49:37,167 --> 01:49:39,538
A place, and everything in it.

1351
01:49:39,937 --> 01:49:40,747
Yes.

1352
01:49:41,148 --> 01:49:42,378
Twenty thousand.

1353
01:49:42,777 --> 01:49:44,259
You have sixty-two.

1354
01:49:44,660 --> 01:49:50,456
And Albert Wesker, who had emptied his pockets in
September and emptied them again in

1355
01:49:50,476 --> 01:49:56,410
March, looked at a balance of sixty-two thousand
Points and did the arithmetic of a man

1356
01:49:56,430 --> 01:49:59,899
who has finally worked out what
game he is playing.

1357
01:50:00,419 --> 01:50:07,445
Sixty. Three pulls of ten, then a fourth. If the
fourth is junk I will still be the only

1358
01:50:07,465 --> 01:50:12,729
man on this planet who can afford to be
disappointed by a miracle.

1359
01:50:13,130 --> 01:50:14,980
The first ten took four minutes.

1360
01:50:15,380 --> 01:50:21,541
Commons: two research associates, a fleet of
vehicles, a cold-chain shipping contract,

1361
01:50:21,561 --> 01:50:27,723
an industrial autoclave. Uncommons: a *Rapid Assay
production licence for the European

1362
01:50:27,743 --> 01:50:33,832
Union*, which Pell would later describe as the
single most valuable piece of paper in

1363
01:50:33,852 --> 01:50:40,229
the building; a cohort of six security officers; a
patent portfolio in protein chemistry.

1364
01:50:40,630 --> 01:50:47,247
Rare, in blue: a second Tyrant, T-103, cradled. It
went into B4 beside the first, and

1365
01:50:47,267 --> 01:50:50,840
Gus filed the service tag
without being asked.

1366
01:50:51,239 --> 01:50:57,589
Rare again: **Ocular Control.** A small thing, and
the only item in three hundred pulls

1367
01:50:57,609 --> 01:51:03,958
that made him genuinely grateful — the ability to
keep his eyes ordinary when the virus

1368
01:51:03,978 --> 01:51:09,229
wanted otherwise. He had worn the glasses for
eight months for a reason.

1369
01:51:09,630 --> 01:51:15,038
The second ten: junk, mostly. The third ten
produced the guaranteed Epic.

1370
01:51:16,188 --> 01:51:24,389
Epic. Anti-Serum Blueprint Line. A universal
countermeasure platform for engineered pathogens.

1371
01:51:24,409 --> 01:51:28,586
Note: this item exists because
you will need it.

1372
01:51:29,176 --> 01:51:30,806
Explain that note.

1373
01:51:31,207 --> 01:51:31,957
No.

1374
01:51:32,357 --> 01:51:37,887
He asked four more times over the following six
years. The answer did not change.

1375
01:51:38,287 --> 01:51:40,526
And then he spent the last
twenty thousand.

1376
01:51:40,926 --> 01:51:47,352
Nine commons. Grey, grey, grey — a reagent lot, a
service contract, three technicians,

1377
01:51:47,372 --> 01:51:53,948
an office lease in Dover he did not need — and Wesker
was already reaching for the panel

1378
01:51:53,968 --> 01:51:56,797
to close it when the room turned gold.

1379
01:51:58,387 --> 01:52:01,607
Legendary. The Arklay Estate.

1380
01:52:02,197 --> 01:52:08,371
The panel did not scroll. It unfolded, line by line,
and the System took its time, because

1381
01:52:08,391 --> 01:52:12,707
the System had a sense of theatre that
it would never admit to.

1382
01:52:13,857 --> 01:52:23,533
The Arklay Estate. A residence of sixty-one rooms in the
Allegheny forest of western Pennsylvania.

1383
01:52:23,553 --> 01:52:32,141
A subsurface complex of nine levels, B S L four
throughout, with a containment hall and

1384
01:52:32,161 --> 01:52:40,848
a cryogenic hall. Dedicated power. Dedicated water. A
private road, a helipad and a rail

1385
01:52:40,868 --> 01:52:49,951
spur. A security detachment of twenty-four, Absolute.
Research staff of forty-one, Absolute.

1386
01:52:49,971 --> 01:52:58,757
Senior personnel: Doctor William Birkin. Doctor
Annette Birkin. Sealed vault, level nine:

1387
01:52:58,777 --> 01:53:00,735
Progenitor. Not yet.

1388
01:53:01,985 --> 01:53:09,130
The narrator will now describe the only time in
sixty-five years that Albert Wesker sat

1389
01:53:09,150 --> 01:53:10,036
down twice.

1390
01:53:10,435 --> 01:53:15,869
He read the last four lines of that panel standing
up. Then he read the two names again.

1391
01:53:15,889 --> 01:53:21,323
Then he put one hand on the back of the leather
chair, and turned it, and sat in it, and

1392
01:53:21,343 --> 01:53:24,855
did not say anything at all for a
minute and ten seconds.

1393
01:53:25,255 --> 01:53:28,115
And the other one, quietly, from the
back of the skull.

1394
01:53:28,635 --> 01:53:29,642
Who are they?

1395
01:53:30,043 --> 01:53:36,203
And for once — the only time, in the entire record —
he answered honestly and immediately.

1396
01:53:36,723 --> 01:53:43,057
They are dead. They died in a city I destroyed, in a
world that is not this one, thirty

1397
01:53:43,077 --> 01:53:45,833
years ago and eighteen years from now.

1398
01:53:46,233 --> 01:53:47,235
Friends?

1399
01:53:47,634 --> 01:53:54,769
No. Nothing so useful. Will was the only mind I
have ever met that I could not simply

1400
01:53:54,789 --> 01:54:02,009
out-work. He was a genius, he was a child, and he
betrayed me, and I betrayed him, and

1401
01:54:02,029 --> 01:54:08,744
both of us thought we had planned it. Annette
hated me accurately for six years.

1402
01:54:09,144 --> 01:54:12,884
And the machine has simply given
them back to you.

1403
01:54:13,283 --> 01:54:20,727
No. It has *issued* them to me. That is not the same
thing, and I intend to keep the difference

1404
01:54:20,747 --> 01:54:23,634
in front of me for as long as I live.

1405
01:54:24,033 --> 01:54:25,684
He flew out at first light.

1406
01:54:26,084 --> 01:54:32,687
The helicopter came in over eleven thousand acres of
Pennsylvania pine, over a private

1407
01:54:32,707 --> 01:54:39,849
road that the state highway department's map showed as
an unimproved service track maintained

1408
01:54:39,869 --> 01:54:45,394
by a timber trust since 1969, and over a gate
with a crimson iris on it.

1409
01:54:45,793 --> 01:54:46,913
And then the house.

1410
01:54:47,314 --> 01:54:53,020
Grey stone, three wings, sixty-one rooms, a slate
roof steep enough to shed a winter,

1411
01:54:53,040 --> 01:54:59,218
and a carriage sweep in front of it with the gravel
raked. Ivy on the west face, established

1412
01:54:59,238 --> 01:55:05,416
— twenty years of ivy. A garage with four cars in it.
Smoke from a chimney, because somebody

1413
01:55:05,436 --> 01:55:09,324
had lit a fire that morning, because
somebody lived there.

1414
01:55:09,723 --> 01:55:15,425
It was not his house from the other world. It was
not the Spencer mansion; it did not

1415
01:55:15,445 --> 01:55:21,684
have the staircase, or the water treatment plant hidden
in the hillside, or the thousand-yard

1416
01:55:21,704 --> 01:55:27,473
gallery of a dead man's vanity. It was a plain,
enormous, unpretentious stone house in

1417
01:55:27,493 --> 01:55:29,963
the wrong forest in the right decade.

1418
01:55:30,363 --> 01:55:35,852
And the way he looked at it, from the air, for ninety
seconds, is the only thing in this

1419
01:55:35,872 --> 01:55:41,172
account the narrator cannot tell you with
certainty, because his face was turned away

1420
01:55:41,192 --> 01:55:43,864
from the only other person in
the aircraft.

1421
01:55:44,264 --> 01:55:50,348
William Birkin came down the steps before the rotors
had stopped, in a lab coat he had

1422
01:55:50,368 --> 01:55:52,904
slept in, holding a tablet, talking.

1423
01:55:53,423 --> 01:55:56,764
Albert. Albert — good, you're here, I need seventeen
minutes and then you can do whatever

1424
01:55:56,784 --> 01:56:00,011
it is you came to do. The cryogenic hall's drawing
four per cent over spec and Annette

1425
01:56:00,031 --> 01:56:03,295
says it's the chillers but it isn't the chillers,
it's that somebody plumbed the return

1426
01:56:03,315 --> 01:56:06,580
on level six through the same riser as the
containment scrubbers, which works, which is

1427
01:56:06,600 --> 01:56:09,865
the problem, because it works until it doesn't and
then we lose forty years of stock in

1428
01:56:09,885 --> 01:56:10,394
an afternoon —

1429
01:56:10,793 --> 01:56:13,363
He stopped, because he had
finally looked up.

1430
01:56:13,884 --> 01:56:15,894
You look strange. Are you ill?
You're never ill.

1431
01:56:16,293 --> 01:56:21,973
Wesker stood on the gravel in front of a man who had
been dead for eighteen years in a

1432
01:56:21,993 --> 01:56:26,944
world that no longer existed, who was talking
to him about a chiller riser.

1433
01:56:27,464 --> 01:56:30,314
How long have you worked for me, Will?

1434
01:56:30,714 --> 01:56:35,443
Six years. Seven in August. Why? Is this an audit
thing? Annette said there'd be an audit

1435
01:56:35,463 --> 01:56:35,764
thing.

1436
01:56:36,163 --> 01:56:36,894
No.

1437
01:56:37,293 --> 01:56:41,887
Good, because I have seven hundred and forty samples
in level nine that I would rather

1438
01:56:41,907 --> 01:56:44,783
not explain to an auditor. Can I
show you the chiller?

1439
01:56:45,183 --> 01:56:51,508
And Albert Wesker — who had tested Marguerite's
obedience with a threat to break her finger,

1440
01:56:51,528 --> 01:56:57,439
who had priced a man's destruction in a boardroom
eleven days earlier and enjoyed it —

1441
01:56:57,459 --> 01:56:57,783
said:

1442
01:56:58,303 --> 01:57:00,593
Yes. Show me the chiller.

1443
01:57:00,994 --> 01:57:04,783
He spent four hours down there. Not one
of them on the chiller.

1444
01:57:05,183 --> 01:57:12,145
Annette Birkin met him at the level-three airlock with
her arms folded and a look he remembered

1445
01:57:12,165 --> 01:57:17,363
so precisely that he had to take a
half-second to arrange his own face.

1446
01:57:17,883 --> 01:57:20,173
You've never come here unannounced before.

1447
01:57:20,572 --> 01:57:21,442
I own it.

1448
01:57:21,842 --> 01:57:26,561
You own a great many things you don't visit.
Whatever you've come to take, take it, and

1449
01:57:26,581 --> 01:57:28,412
don't let him give you level nine.

1450
01:57:28,812 --> 01:57:29,663
Why not?

1451
01:57:30,062 --> 01:57:35,418
Because he'll give you anything you ask for, Dr.
Wesker. He always has. Someone in this

1452
01:57:35,438 --> 01:57:40,855
house has to say no to you, and it is not going to be
him, and it is certainly not going

1453
01:57:40,875 --> 01:57:45,613
to be the forty-one people downstairs who agree
with everything anybody says.

1454
01:57:46,013 --> 01:57:53,296
The narrator notes that Annette Birkin was, per the
System's own roster, Absolute — incapable

1455
01:57:53,316 --> 01:57:56,123
of disobedience, incapable of doubt.

1456
01:57:56,523 --> 01:58:00,682
And she was standing in a doorway telling him
to be careful with her husband.

1457
01:58:01,082 --> 01:58:07,241
Wesker filed that. He filed it very carefully
indeed, and it is the first crack in the

1458
01:58:07,261 --> 01:58:13,132
story of what the Absolute actually are, and it
does not open again for six years.

1459
01:58:13,533 --> 01:58:14,603
Nine levels.

1460
01:58:15,003 --> 01:58:21,052
Containment wings on three and four. A biologics
suite on five that made Division Seven's

1461
01:58:21,072 --> 01:58:27,053
B4 look like a school laboratory. On six, an animal
facility with things in it that Voss

1462
01:58:27,073 --> 01:58:30,873
would have needed a year of ethics
paperwork to look at.

1463
01:58:31,273 --> 01:58:33,693
And on seven, the cryogenic hall.

1464
01:58:34,092 --> 01:58:40,164
It was the size of a cathedral nave and it was mostly
empty, which was somehow worse than

1465
01:58:40,184 --> 01:58:46,324
full. Four hundred and eleven cradles in ranks, of
which nine were occupied: six by tissue

1466
01:58:46,344 --> 01:58:52,142
banks, two by the T-103s he had shipped up that
morning from Wilmington, and one — at

1467
01:58:52,162 --> 01:58:58,233
the far end, alone, under a light that was slightly
brighter than the others — by nothing

1468
01:58:58,253 --> 01:58:58,713
at all.

1469
01:58:59,232 --> 01:59:01,362
What is that cradle for?

1470
01:59:01,763 --> 01:59:06,471
I don't know. It was in the drawings. It's plumbed and
powered and it's rated for something

1471
01:59:06,491 --> 01:59:08,913
a great deal heavier than a
Tyrant. I've asked.

1472
01:59:09,312 --> 01:59:10,682
