10:33:53,691 --> 10:34:00,969
Umbrella's share of the human economy in 2268:
nought point four per cent.

7573
10:34:01,369 --> 10:34:07,810
The narrator notes that this is the number Albert
Wesker spent the second half of his

7574
10:34:07,830 --> 10:34:14,423
life producing, that no history before 2350 treats
it as an achievement, and that it is

7575
10:34:14,443 --> 10:34:20,199
the only figure in this entire account that he
is recorded as having framed.

7576
10:34:20,599 --> 10:34:27,805
He went back to Wilmington and to the valley about
twice a decade, and the narrator has

7577
10:34:27,825 --> 10:34:35,114
a favourite entry in the Red Queen's log, from the
two hundred and twentieth anniversary

7578
10:34:35,134 --> 10:34:35,778
in 2246.

7579
10:34:36,178 --> 10:34:42,616
He stood at the back of a public meeting in Raccoon
City about a tram extension, for three

7580
10:34:42,636 --> 10:34:49,002
hours, and nobody recognised him, because the
sunglasses had gone out of fashion in about

7581
10:34:49,022 --> 10:34:55,316
2180 and because there were four hundred thousand
people in that valley by then and most

7582
10:34:55,336 --> 10:34:58,689
of them had never seen a
photograph of anybody.

7583
10:34:59,089 --> 10:35:05,047
The tram extension was voted down. He is recorded
as having voted against it, because

7584
10:35:05,067 --> 10:35:11,026
he had been on the electoral roll of that city
since 2081 and had never once missed a

7585
10:35:11,046 --> 10:35:11,519
ballot.

7586
10:35:12,039 --> 10:35:14,305
You voted against the extension.

7587
10:35:14,705 --> 10:35:21,267
The costing was dishonest. Four hundred and eleven
million for nine kilometres, and the

7588
10:35:21,287 --> 10:35:23,386
survey was eleven years old.

7589
10:35:23,787 --> 10:35:29,416
You could have funded it out of the Directorate's
contingency without anybody noticing.

7590
10:35:29,816 --> 10:35:36,024
(very cold) Yes. And then a hundred and forty
thousand people would have got a tram they

7591
10:35:36,044 --> 10:35:41,826
voted against, from a man they have never met,
because he thought they were wrong.

7592
10:35:42,436 --> 10:35:46,126
They were wrong. That is what a
vote is for.

7593
10:35:46,527 --> 10:35:52,533
The Red Queen's log records that she spent four
hours after that conversation reviewing

7594
10:35:52,553 --> 10:35:58,491
one hundred and twelve entries on a list she had
kept since 2043, and closed the file,

7595
10:35:58,511 --> 10:35:59,877
and did not say why.

7596
10:36:00,277 --> 10:36:07,347
Evelyn Marsh's daughter Mira Reiss-Marsh turned one
hundred and seventy-five in 2264 and

7597
10:36:07,366 --> 10:36:14,275
had, by then, four children, nineteen grandchildren,
and a stated policy of never once

7598
10:36:14,295 --> 10:36:21,527
accepting a position of any kind, which she described
as *the family trade being avoided*.

7599
10:36:21,926 --> 10:36:28,417
The Progenitor lineage numbered four hundred and
eleven thousand people across nine hundred

7600
10:36:28,437 --> 10:36:34,713
worlds. They do not age. They cannot be infected.
They are, in every measurable respect,

7601
10:36:34,733 --> 10:36:41,009
a separate and permanent branch of the species, and
the argument the Red Queen predicted

7602
10:36:41,029 --> 10:36:47,519
in 2089 has been running for a hundred and
seventy-five years and has not produced a single

7603
10:36:47,539 --> 10:36:54,101
death, which is the most surprising fact in this
account and which nobody has satisfactorily

7604
10:36:54,121 --> 10:36:54,816
explained.

7605
10:36:55,216 --> 10:37:01,989
Evelyn herself had stopped working in 2211 — the
year the Myriad archive was opened and

7606
10:37:02,009 --> 10:37:08,626
the year she wrote the four sentences about the
glacier — and had spent the following

7607
10:37:08,646 --> 10:37:12,686
half-century on the ice doing
nothing in particular.

7608
10:37:13,206 --> 10:37:14,476
Fifty years.

7609
10:37:14,877 --> 10:37:20,666
Fifty-three. I'm not bored, if that's the question. I
read. I have a great many grandchildren

7610
10:37:20,686 --> 10:37:24,727
who visit and a great many who do not,
which is how it should be.

7611
10:37:25,336 --> 10:37:26,126
Are you bored?

7612
10:37:26,527 --> 10:37:31,957
(very cold) I have been bored
since about 2160.

7613
10:37:32,357 --> 10:37:38,287
The narrator notes that this is the only complaint
Albert Wesker makes in two hundred

7614
10:37:38,307 --> 10:37:39,897
and thirty-eight years.

7615
10:37:40,416 --> 10:37:41,441
Then do something.

7616
10:37:41,843 --> 10:37:49,075
There is nothing to do. That is not self-pity; it is
an accurate description. The species

7617
10:37:49,095 --> 10:37:56,001
is nineteen billion people who argue, the border is
quiet, the Assembly does not need

7618
10:37:56,021 --> 10:38:02,927
me, the Directorate is the ninth largest supplier
of anything, and every problem I am

7619
10:38:02,947 --> 10:38:10,098
equipped for has been solved by people who are better
at their own lives than I ever was

7620
10:38:10,118 --> 10:38:10,913
at theirs.

7621
10:38:11,192 --> 10:38:17,656
(very soft) I built a world that does not require me.
It took two hundred and thirty-eight

7622
10:38:17,676 --> 10:38:21,402
years and it is the only thing I
have ever finished.

7623
10:38:22,353 --> 10:38:24,443
Albert. There's a panel, isn't there.

7624
10:38:25,282 --> 10:38:32,932
(very cold) There has been a panel since
2262. I have not opened it.

7625
10:38:33,333 --> 10:38:34,963
How long has it been sitting there?

7626
10:38:35,363 --> 10:38:38,853
Two years, one month and eleven days.

7627
10:38:39,253 --> 10:38:44,735
She looked at him for a long moment on an ice shelf
at dusk, the second oldest thing on

7628
10:38:44,755 --> 10:38:46,823
the planet looking at the oldest.

7629
10:38:47,343 --> 10:38:48,454
You're frightened of it.

7630
10:38:48,853 --> 10:38:55,482
(very cold) I am two hundred and thirty-eight years
old, I have been shot four hundred

7631
10:38:55,502 --> 10:39:02,440
times, I have boarded a hive, and I have been declared
noise by a theocracy that has ended

7632
10:39:02,460 --> 10:39:03,523
three species.

7633
10:39:18,365 --> 10:39:25,841
The ship that arrived at Ochoa in March of 2268 was
not Veth, was not Compact, was not

7634
10:39:25,861 --> 10:39:31,855
Choir, and was not anything in nine hundred
light years of catalogue.

7635
10:39:32,256 --> 10:39:38,454
It was four kilometres long and it was welded. Human
eyes could see that from the approach

7636
10:39:38,474 --> 10:39:44,534
telemetry: four million hand welds, the ribs of a
hull built in a yard by people arguing

7637
10:39:44,554 --> 10:39:50,545
about a budget, and a drive geometry that the human
sphere had been using for a hundred

7638
10:39:50,565 --> 10:39:54,345
and seventy-six years and had
believed nobody else had.

7639
10:39:54,745 --> 10:40:00,736
It broadcast on approach, in the clear, in a language
nobody had heard, and the Compact's

7640
10:40:00,756 --> 10:40:06,612
translation array had it in nine hours because the
transmission carried its own primer,

7641
10:40:06,632 --> 10:40:09,516
built the way a patient
teacher builds one.

7642
10:40:09,916 --> 10:40:14,056
And the first sentence, when it came out,
stopped four hundred worlds.

7643
10:40:14,456 --> 10:40:19,789
It was eleven words long, and every account of the
next twenty years begins by quoting

7644
10:40:19,809 --> 10:40:19,976
it.

7645
10:40:20,826 --> 10:40:25,517
We are looking for the one who published the
catalogue. We have one too.

7646
10:40:25,916 --> 10:40:31,833
Albert Wesker had been waiting for this conversation
since the nineteenth of March 2051,

7647
10:40:31,853 --> 10:40:38,040
when a panel in an empty chamber had told him there had
been four hundred and eleven worlds,

7648
10:40:38,060 --> 10:40:43,909
that four hundred and one had failed, and that of
the nine that had not, *two are still

7649
10:40:43,929 --> 10:40:44,516
running.*

7650
10:40:44,916 --> 10:40:50,032
He had asked what that meant for two hundred and
seventeen years. He had been told the

7651
10:40:50,052 --> 10:40:51,167
panel was accurate.

7652
10:40:51,567 --> 10:40:57,187
The species aboard that ship call themselves a word
the array renders as the Ninth, which

7653
10:40:57,207 --> 10:41:02,764
is not a name but a position in a list, and which
they had taken from their own System's

7654
10:41:02,784 --> 10:41:07,137
ledger, and which tells you most of what
you need to know about them.

7655
10:41:07,537 --> 10:41:13,036
Their host — the individual their System had
chosen, three thousand one hundred years

7656
10:41:13,056 --> 10:41:18,815
ago, and who had been alive ever since — was a person
the record calls Tesh, of a species

7657
10:41:18,835 --> 10:41:24,658
with four sexes and no equivalent word for
*ownership*, and Tesh had spent three millennia

7658
10:41:24,678 --> 10:41:30,437
building something that the human record has never
been able to describe without sounding

7659
10:41:30,457 --> 10:41:31,086
sarcastic.

7660
10:41:31,486 --> 10:41:33,096
Tesh had built a library.

7661
10:41:33,616 --> 10:41:36,241
You are much younger than I expected.

7662
10:41:36,641 --> 10:41:39,311
Two hundred and forty-two years.

7663
10:41:39,711 --> 10:41:45,999
Yes. Very young. I was four hundred before I
understood what the catalogue was doing to

7664
10:41:46,019 --> 10:41:49,334
me, and twenty-three hundred
before I stopped.

7665
10:41:50,055 --> 10:41:56,312
You published it at one hundred and eighty-six. That
is why we came. Our System showed

7666
10:41:56,332 --> 10:42:02,662
us your world's ledger entry nine years ago — it
does not usually show us other worlds;

7667
10:42:02,682 --> 10:42:08,867
it showed us that one — and the entry said: *the
interface has been published and the

7668
10:42:08,887 --> 10:42:10,400
host was not killed.*

7669
10:42:10,680 --> 10:42:15,694
(very slow) In three thousand one hundred years I
have never seen it volunteer anything.

7670
10:42:16,094 --> 10:42:21,932
The narrator wants to be exact about the next part,
because it is the answer to the question

7671
10:42:21,952 --> 10:42:24,924
this account has been asking
since Chapter One.

7672
10:42:25,444 --> 10:42:27,354
(very cold) What is it?

7673
10:42:27,754 --> 10:42:34,396
I do not know. I have asked for three thousand years
in four hundred ways. I have built

7674
10:42:34,416 --> 10:42:41,669
instruments. I have had a civilisation of nine billion
people looking for it for two millennia.

7675
10:42:41,689 --> 10:42:44,042
We have never located anything.

7676
10:42:44,323 --> 10:42:50,303
But I know what it *does*, because I have watched it
do it to me, and because you have

7677
10:42:50,323 --> 10:42:56,652
now published eleven thousand pages that match my own
record almost exactly, in a different

7678
10:42:56,672 --> 10:43:00,629
language, on a different world, four
hundred years apart.

7679
10:43:01,460 --> 10:43:06,428
(very slow) It is looking for something, and it has
not found it, and it has been looking

7680
10:43:06,448 --> 10:43:07,604
for a very long time.

7681
10:43:08,005 --> 10:43:09,295
Looking for what?

7682
10:43:09,695 --> 10:43:15,792
Consider what it pays for. Not conquest — my world
was never conquered; I bought nine

7683
10:43:15,812 --> 10:43:21,981
hundred libraries and it paid me for every one. Not
kindness; it paid the tyrants too,

7684
10:43:22,001 --> 10:43:28,098
the record is explicit. It pays for *consequence*.
For a will that makes a difference

7685
10:43:28,118 --> 10:43:30,977
that would not otherwise have been made.

7686
10:43:31,256 --> 10:43:35,909
And consider what it does when the will
stops mattering. It leaves.

7687
10:43:36,188 --> 10:43:41,245
(very slow) Four hundred and one worlds failed. Not
because the host died — because the

7688
10:43:41,265 --> 10:43:46,380
host *arrived*. Because he took the catalogue and
built a thing so complete that nothing

7689
10:43:46,400 --> 10:43:51,340
further would ever happen there, and the ledger
went quiet, and the System closed the

7690
10:43:51,360 --> 10:43:52,449
world and moved on.

7691
10:43:53,508 --> 10:43:58,529
Albert Wesker sat very still in a room in
orbit above the Earth.

7692
10:43:59,049 --> 10:44:04,917
(very cold) It is not measuring achievement. It is
measuring whether anything is still

7693
10:44:04,937 --> 10:44:05,739
*happening*.

7694
10:44:06,138 --> 10:44:12,655
That is my conclusion after three thousand years. I
could be wrong. I have been wrong

7695
10:44:12,675 --> 10:44:14,193
about it four times.

7696
10:44:14,592 --> 10:44:21,021
And the ninth world? Yours is the ninth in its
list, and mine is the four hundred and

7697
10:44:21,041 --> 10:44:24,282
eleventh. What happened to the
other seven?

7698
10:44:24,683 --> 10:44:29,974
(very slow) They are still there. All seven.
Perfect, stable, immortal, and nothing has

7699
10:44:29,994 --> 10:44:35,347
happened in any of them for between four hundred and
nine thousand years. I have visited

7700
10:44:35,367 --> 10:44:35,714
three.

7701
10:44:36,433 --> 10:44:42,603
They are the most beautiful places I have ever
seen, and there is nobody home, in the

7702
10:44:42,623 --> 10:44:43,987
sense that matters.

7703
10:44:44,387 --> 10:44:51,207
Tesh took a human survey ship to one of the seven,
which no human being had ever seen

7704
10:44:51,227 --> 10:44:56,197
and which the Compact had been quietly
cataloguing since 2212.

7705
10:44:56,597 --> 10:45:00,106
The narrator will describe it once and
then leave it alone.

7706
10:45:00,506 --> 10:45:06,556
It is a world of nine billion people. They are
alive. They are not asleep, or frozen,

7707
10:45:06,576 --> 10:45:12,625
or in any way preserved: they eat, they work, they
raise children, and they have done

7708
10:45:12,645 --> 10:45:18,766
so for four thousand one hundred years in a
civilisation that is, by every measure the

7709
10:45:18,786 --> 10:45:25,478
human survey team could apply, perfect. No hunger. No
disease. No violence — not low violence;

7710
10:45:25,498 --> 10:45:31,762
none, for four millennia. No poverty, no pollution,
no crime, and no government, because

7711
10:45:31,782 --> 10:45:34,046
there is nothing left to govern.

7712
10:45:34,446 --> 10:45:40,495
Their host is still there. He is four thousand years
old, he is the most beloved individual

7713
10:45:40,515 --> 10:45:46,164
in the recorded history of any species, and he
lives alone in a house in a garden and

7714
10:45:46,184 --> 10:45:50,965
has not been asked for anything since the
year their calendar calls Six.

7715
10:45:51,366 --> 10:45:56,944
The survey team spent eleven days there. Everybody
was kind to them. Nothing was hidden.

7716
10:45:56,964 --> 10:46:02,669
And every single member of that team asked to leave
early, and the ship's surgeon's report

7717
10:46:02,689 --> 10:46:08,076
is four lines and ends: *There is nothing wrong
with these people. I want that in the

7718
10:46:08,096 --> 10:46:13,929
record. I also want it recorded that I have never in my
life wanted so badly to be somewhere

7719
10:46:13,949 --> 10:46:15,456
with an argument in it.*

7720
10:46:15,976 --> 10:46:18,537
That is what it looks like when it works.

7721
10:46:18,936 --> 10:46:21,366
(very cold) It is a *waiting room*.

7722
10:46:21,767 --> 10:46:27,856
It is a finished thing. You built one too, on a
platform in an ocean, with four hundred

7723
10:46:27,876 --> 10:46:33,965
thousand people in it who could not think of
anything to do, and your own report called

7724
10:46:33,985 --> 10:46:38,600
it a consequence deficit, and you opened a
frontier because of it.

7725
10:46:38,880 --> 10:46:43,814
(very slow) The difference between your world and
that one is not that you were wiser.

7726
10:46:43,834 --> 10:46:47,156
It is that yours was still small
enough to notice in time.

7727
10:46:47,556 --> 10:46:49,200
They talked for five months.

7728
10:46:49,601 --> 10:46:54,702
The narrator will give you the four things that came
out of it, because between them they

7729
10:46:54,722 --> 10:46:56,371
are the ending of this story.

7730
10:46:56,770 --> 10:47:03,205
The first: the Ninth and the human sphere exchanged
everything. Not treaties — *records*.

7731
10:47:03,225 --> 10:47:09,441
Three thousand one hundred years of Tesh's ledger
against two hundred and forty-two of

7732
10:47:09,461 --> 10:47:15,460
Wesker's, published in full on both sides, to
every species in reach, in the clear.

7733
10:47:15,861 --> 10:47:21,888
The second: the Compact, which had been told about
the catalogue in 2212 and had been

7734
10:47:21,908 --> 10:47:27,935
quietly looking ever since, found four more. Not
hosts — *former* worlds. Four of the

7735
10:47:27,955 --> 10:47:34,053
four hundred and one failures, dead systems with
impossible architecture in them, in a

7736
10:47:34,073 --> 10:47:38,891
volume nobody had surveyed because there
was nothing there to trade.

7737
10:47:39,291 --> 10:47:45,032
The third: Tesh stayed. Three thousand one hundred
years old, and she settled on Ochoa,

7738
10:47:45,052 --> 10:47:50,927
in a house, near a colony of nine hundred thousand
people who argued constantly, and when

7739
10:47:50,947 --> 10:47:56,821
asked why she said it was the first place she had
been in two millennia where the outcome

7740
10:47:56,841 --> 10:47:57,881
was not obvious.

7741
10:47:58,281 --> 10:48:04,197
And the fourth is a question that Albert Wesker
asked in the last week, and the answer

7742
10:48:04,217 --> 10:48:08,960
he got, and it is the reason he did what he
does in the next chapter.

7743
10:48:09,480 --> 10:48:15,500
(very cold) You have had three thousand one hundred
years and you have never been offered

7744
10:48:15,520 --> 10:48:16,450
a final panel.

7745
10:48:16,850 --> 10:48:17,662
No.

7746
10:48:18,061 --> 10:48:26,372
I have one. It has been open since
2262. I have not read it.

7747
10:48:27,321 --> 10:48:29,585
(very slow) Then it is finishing with you.

7748
10:48:29,864 --> 10:48:35,696
That is not a threat and I am not being poetic. In
my record there are nine hosts who

7749
10:48:35,716 --> 10:48:41,685
reached what you would call the sixth tier. Seven of
them were offered something at the

7750
10:48:41,705 --> 10:48:46,642
end that I have only ever seen described,
because the descriptions stop.

7751
10:48:46,922 --> 10:48:52,991
It comes when the world no longer needs the host.
Yours does not need you. Mine still

7752
10:48:53,011 --> 10:48:59,367
needs me, which I have come to understand is not a
compliment: it means I failed to build

7753
10:48:59,387 --> 10:49:03,522
something that could continue without
me, and you did not.

7754
10:49:04,362 --> 10:49:06,752
(very soft) What does it offer?

7755
10:49:07,152 --> 10:49:12,744
(very slow) I do not know, Albert Wesker. But I know
what it costs, because it is written

7756
10:49:12,764 --> 10:49:16,465
in the terms and neither of us has
ever read them properly.

7757
10:49:16,744 --> 10:49:22,955
*What is given is given.* Everything it gave you
stays. The cities, the medicine, the

7758
10:49:22,975 --> 10:49:28,599
drive, the lineage, the nineteen billion
people. It does not take those back.

7759
10:49:44,948 --> 10:49:52,496
He read it on the fourteenth of September 2286,
twenty-four years after it appeared, two

7760
10:49:52,516 --> 10:49:59,892
hundred and sixty years to the day after a building
finished existing on a car park in

7761
10:49:59,912 --> 10:50:01,699
Wilmington, Delaware.

7762
10:50:02,098 --> 10:50:08,715
He read it in the library at Arklay, in the house
that had come out of a lottery in 2027

7763
10:50:08,735 --> 10:50:13,768
with two ghosts in it, alone, at two
minutes to six in the morning.

7764
10:50:14,919 --> 10:50:23,984
Final panel. Host: Albert Wesker. World: Earth,
designation baseline. Tier six, Stellar.

7765
10:50:24,004 --> 10:50:33,173
Duration of tenure: two hundred and sixty years.
Assessment: the world no longer requires

7766
10:50:33,193 --> 10:50:34,102
the host.

7767
10:50:34,692 --> 10:50:36,882
(very cold) Say the offer.

7768
10:50:37,913 --> 10:50:44,904
Offer. Ascension. The host is removed from the
world and continues. What continues is

7769
10:50:44,924 --> 10:50:52,163
not a man. It is the will, without the body, without
the species, without the place. You

7770
10:50:52,183 --> 10:50:59,340
would be as I am, and you would carry a catalogue,
and you would go to a world that has

7771
10:50:59,360 --> 10:51:05,197
none. Term: indefinite. Precedent: seven
hosts accepted. None declined.

7772
10:51:06,557 --> 10:51:12,976
The narrator asks you to understand what had just been
said, because it took Albert Wesker

7773
10:51:12,996 --> 10:51:17,627
about four seconds and it has taken
historians two hundred years.

7774
10:51:18,027 --> 10:51:23,397
The System was not offering to make him a god.
It was offering him its own job.

7775
10:51:23,917 --> 10:51:25,987
(very soft) You were a host.

7776
10:51:26,386 --> 10:51:27,513
