7979
11:08:39,048 --> 11:08:43,941
— and that's the bit everyone remembers. But
somebody tell me the important part. Why

7980
11:08:43,961 --> 11:08:45,038
is it the building?

7981
11:08:45,439 --> 11:08:47,119
Because it just appeared.

7982
11:08:47,519 --> 11:08:49,109
It did. Anyone else?

7983
11:08:49,509 --> 11:08:51,289
Because nobody noticed.

7984
11:08:52,129 --> 11:08:57,238
Yes. That's the one. Eight billion people, and the
buildings and the cities and the medicine

7985
11:08:57,258 --> 11:09:02,312
and the ships all arrived, and nobody noticed, because
they came with the paperwork already

7986
11:09:02,332 --> 11:09:02,591
done.

7987
11:09:02,870 --> 11:09:07,747
And it took this species two hundred years to work
out that the dangerous part was never

7988
11:09:07,767 --> 11:09:11,140
the man. It was that we are extremely
easy to hand things to.

7989
11:09:11,541 --> 11:09:17,211
The narrator will stop editorialising now and
simply set down the last four facts.

7990
11:09:17,610 --> 11:09:25,573
Albert Wesker died on the eleventh of June 2294, at
Wilmington, aged two hundred and sixty-eight,

7991
11:09:25,593 --> 11:09:32,569
of a cerebral haemorrhage, which is an ordinary
thing that happens to ordinary people

7992
11:09:32,589 --> 11:09:40,305
and which no hospital on earth was able to prevent
because by then he was, in every measurable

7993
11:09:40,325 --> 11:09:42,609
respect, an ordinary person.

7994
11:09:43,010 --> 11:09:48,882
He left instructions of four lines. No state
funeral, no monument, no name on anything

7995
11:09:48,902 --> 11:09:54,912
that is not already carrying it. He was buried in the
cemetery on the hill above Raccoon

7996
11:09:54,932 --> 11:10:00,736
City, in the section for the people who were made
there, among eleven thousand graves

7997
11:10:00,756 --> 11:10:06,628
that had been dug for people who never existed until
a Saturday in 2028, and his stone

7998
11:10:06,648 --> 11:10:08,478
is the same size as theirs.

7999
11:10:08,879 --> 11:10:15,705
The Umbrella Directorate flew its flag at half mast
for the third and last time, and then,

8000
11:10:15,725 --> 11:10:22,551
in 2301, dissolved itself by a vote of its own board,
and its assets went to the Assembly,

8001
11:10:22,571 --> 11:10:29,397
and the argument about what to do with them ran for
forty years and was, by every account,

8002
11:10:29,417 --> 11:10:30,310
magnificent.

8003
11:10:30,709 --> 11:10:32,290
And the last fact is this.

8004
11:10:32,690 --> 11:10:38,312
In four hundred and eleven worlds, an entity that
has never been located and never been

8005
11:10:38,332 --> 11:10:43,825
described gave four hundred and eleven people a
catalogue with prices in it, and paid

8006
11:10:43,845 --> 11:10:49,468
them in the love of the populations they made, and
four hundred and one of those worlds

8007
11:10:49,488 --> 11:10:51,219
became perfect and stopped.

8008
11:10:51,619 --> 11:10:57,744
Seven are beautiful and silent. One is a library
that ends when its librarian does.

8009
11:10:58,145 --> 11:11:03,814
And one is a loud, argumentative, badly governed
sphere of nineteen billion people spread

8010
11:11:03,834 --> 11:11:09,503
across nine hundred worlds, who found out exactly
what had been done to them and to their

8011
11:11:09,523 --> 11:11:15,000
grandparents, and did not forgive it, and did not
tear it down either, and are still —

8012
11:11:15,020 --> 11:11:20,753
four centuries after the last panel went out in an
office on the sixth floor of a building

8013
11:11:20,773 --> 11:11:24,525
that was always there — shouting at
each other about trams.

8014
11:11:25,474 --> 11:11:27,285
That is the one that kept going.

8015
11:11:27,685 --> 11:11:34,326
The narrator would like to record, finally, that
nobody in it needed Albert Wesker to
